{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014K72KPyWckDwEAtfYFYd7f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Chu & Stuckey's (revised) algorithm\n2\t\n3\tSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4) in paper form. It distils\n4\tthe working report `search_soundness.md` (Ralph loop0006, items 05 to 13),\n5\twhich holds the code references, the full check tables and the history. Every\n6\tstatement here is proved in Lean unless it says otherwise. The Lean names\n7\trefer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, namespace\n8\t`MOSPFormalization.Search`: seven files (`Basic`, `DefiniteMove`,\n9\t`SubsetRule`, `BetterMove`, `Memo`, `Decide`, `DefiniteMatching`), with no\n10\t`sorry` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n11\t(`axiom_check.lean`). Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the\n12\tpreprint `../literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 4.1 Introduction\n17\t\n18\tLet $M$ be a MOSP instance and $k \\ge 0$ an integer. The algorithm of this\n19\tsection decides whether some sequence of the products keeps at most $k$\n20\tcustomer stacks open at once, that is, whether $Z(M) \\le k$. By (E2) of\n21\tsection 3 (Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`), $Z(M) \\le k$ holds exactly\n22\twhen the MOSP graph $G_M$ has pathwidth at most $k - 1$. So the algorithm is\n23\talso a decision procedure for pathwidth. Any graph $G$ is the MOSP graph of the\n24\tinstance with one customer per vertex and one product per edge, each product\n25\tneeded by the two ends of its edge.\n26\t\n27\tThe algorithm is the *customer search* of Chu & Stuckey (2009). It searches\n28\tover the orders in which the customers' stacks close, not over product\n29\tsequences, and it prunes the search with several dominance rules. Chu &\n30\tStuckey state three of these rules as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and\n31\ta fourth, the subset rule, in their §2.\n32\t\n33\t**Two of the published theorems are false as stated.** Theorem 1, the\n34\t*definite move*, can discard the last solution at a node. A 14-customer graph\n35\tshows it, and Lean proves the counterexample. Theorem 2, the *better move*,\n36\trests on Theorem 1 applied one step deeper, and it inherits the fault. Its\n37\tform as the code has run it since 2026-09-26 is still false.\n38\t\n39\t**The revised algorithm** keeps the search and every rule. It replaces the\n40\tpremises of Theorems 1 and 2 by a stronger, *hereditary* premise. That premise\n41\tis proved sound, and it is proved equivalent to a bipartite matching\n42\tcondition, so it costs one small matching per candidate. With the two repairs,\n43\tthe whole search is proved sound in Lean: a refutation at $k$ means\n44\t$Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$.\n45\t\n46\tNo wrong answer of the unrepaired code on a whole instance is known. The gap\n47\tis at the level of a node, and the search has so far always recovered through\n48\tanother branch. Section 4.4.4 states exactly what is proved for the code as it\n49\tstands.\n50\t\n51\t**The owner's decision** (2026-10-01, `plan.md`, *Decisions*). This paper\n52\tstates the gap and the repaired rule. Both solvers, the MOSP customer search\n53\tand the graph pathwidth solver, will be changed to implement the repaired\n54\trules, and the values that rest only on the customer search will then be\n55\tre-certified.\n56\t\n57\t---\n58\t\n59\t## 4.2 The search as a mathematical object\n60\t\n61\t### 4.2.1 Definitions\n62\t\n63\tChu & Stuckey observe that the products matter only through the MOSP graph:\n64\t\"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the same minimum\n65\tnumber of stacks\" (PDF p. 4). So fix a finite simple graph $G = (C, E)$ whose\n66\tvertices are the customers, and an integer $k \\ge 0$. For a customer $c$ and a\n67\tset $T \\subseteq C$, write\n68\t\n69\t- $N[c] = \\{c\\} \\cup \\{d : cd \\in E\\}$, the closed neighbourhood: the stacks\n70\t  that must be open when $c$'s stack closes;\n71\t- $O(T) = \\bigcup_{c \\in T} N[c]$, the stacks *opened* once the customers of\n72\t  $T$ have closed, with $O(\\emptyset) = \\emptyset$;\n73\t- $\\mathrm{fin}(X) = \\{c : N[c] \\subseteq X\\}$, the customers that could close\n74\t  at no cost once the stacks $X$ are open;\n75\t- $\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{fin}(O(T))$, the *free closure* of $T$;\n76\t- $b(T) = |O(T) \\setminus T|$, the stacks open after $T$ has closed;\n77\t- $o(c, T) = N[c] \\setminus O(T)$, the stacks that closing $c$ next would newly\n78\t  open, and $\\mathrm{open}(c, T) = |o(c, T)|$.\n79\t\n80\tTwo facts are immediate: $T \\subseteq \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ and\n81\t$O(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) = O(T)$ (Lean: `subset_cl`, `opened_cl`). The second says\n82\tthat free closing opens nothing.\n83\t\n84\t**Moves and costs.** A *move* at $T$ closes one customer $c \\notin T$. Its cost\n85\tis the number of stacks open at that moment, counting $c$ itself:\n86\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = |O(T \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus T| = b(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1.$$\n87\tThis is Chu & Stuckey's $|O(S) - S \\cup o(c, S)|$ (PDF p. 4). The move is\n88\t*playable* when $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) \\le k$. A *closing order from* $T$ is an\n89\tenumeration $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ of $C \\setminus T$. Its cost is the largest of\n90\tits step costs $\\mathrm{cost}(T \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_{i-1}\\}, c_i)$, and $0$\n91\twhen $m = 0$. Write $P_k(T)$ when some closing order from $T$ costs at most\n92\t$k$. (Lean: `stepCost`, `orderCost`, `Solvable`;\n93\t`stepCost_eq_openStacks_insert_add_one` for the second form of the cost.)\n94\t\n95\t**States.** On entering a node, the search closes every customer whose\n96\tneighbourhood is already open. One pass suffices, because closing such a\n97\tcustomer opens nothing. So the search only ever stands at a *state*, a set\n98\t$S$ with $S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$. The root $\\emptyset$ is a state, since every\n99\t$N[c]$ is nonempty. The *child* of a state $S$ by $c \\notin S$ is\n100\t$$S \\cdot c = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{c\\}).$$\n101\t\n102\t**The decision predicate.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ holds when $S = C$, or when some\n103\t$c \\notin S$ has $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$ and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n104\tThis is the question the search answers: \"is there a run of playable moves,\n105\teach followed by its free moves, that closes everyone?\" (Lean: `SearchSol`,\n106\tdefined inductively and not through $P_k$, so that the link between the two is\n107\ta theorem.)\n108\t\n109\t**The invariant.** Every node the search visits satisfies\n110\t$$b(S) = |O(S) \\setminus S| \\le k.$$\n111\tIt holds at the root, where $b(\\emptyset) = 0$. At a child it holds because\n112\t$b(S \\cdot c) \\le |O(S \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{c\\})| =\n113\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$ (Lean: `card_opened_insert_sdiff_lt`). Several\n114\tresults below need it, and it is never an extra assumption about the search.\n115\t\n116\t### 4.2.2 Lemma F: free moves never hurt\n117\t\n118\t**Lemma 4.1 (monotonicity).** If $T \\subseteq T'$ and $O(T') \\subseteq O(T)$,\n119\tthen $P_k(T) \\Rightarrow P_k(T')$.\n120\t\n121\t*Proof.* Take a closing order from $T$ of cost at most $k$ and delete the\n122\tcustomers of $T'$ from it. Each remaining step closes some $c$ at a prefix\n123\t$T' \\cup A$ in place of $T \\cup A$. Its opened set\n124\t$O(T' \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\}) \\subseteq O(T \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\})$ is no larger, and the\n125\tset removed from it, $T' \\cup A \\supseteq T \\cup A$, is no smaller. So no step\n126\tcosts more. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_mono`, via `orderCost_filter_le`.)\n127\t\n128\t**Lemma 4.2 (Lemma F).** If $b(T) \\le k$, then\n129\t$P_k(T) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$. The direction ($\\Rightarrow$)\n130\tneeds no hypothesis. At the root, $P_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$.\n131\t\n132\t*Proof.* ($\\Rightarrow$) By Lemma 4.1 with $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T)$, it suffices\n133\tto show $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ for a state $S$. Induct on\n134\t$|C \\setminus S|$. If $S \\ne C$, let $c$ be the first move of an order from\n135\t$S$ of cost at most $k$. Then $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$, the rest of the\n136\torder gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, Lemma 4.1 gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$, and\n137\tinduction gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n138\t\n139\t($\\Leftarrow$) First, $P_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$ when\n140\t$b(T) \\le k$. Close the customers of $\\mathrm{cl}(T) \\setminus T$ one at a\n141\ttime. Each step at a prefix $U$ with $T \\subseteq U$ costs\n142\t$|O(T) \\setminus U| \\le b(T) \\le k$, because the customer closed lies in\n143\t$O(T)$ already. Then follow the order from $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n144\tSecond, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(S)$ at a state with the invariant,\n145\tby induction on $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. At a move $c$, the child $S \\cdot c$\n146\tsatisfies the invariant, so induction gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$. The first step\n147\tthen gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, because $b(S \\cup \\{c\\}) =\n148\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$. Prepending $c$, at cost at most $k$, gives\n149\t$P_k(S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`,\n150\t`searchSol_cl_of_solvable`, `solvable_of_searchSol`, `solvable_cl_iff`;\n151\tat the root, `solvable_empty_iff`.)\n152\t\n153\tThe hypothesis is needed. On $K_2$ with one customer closed and $k = 0$,\n154\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ is everything, so $\\mathrm{Sol}_0(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$ holds, but\n155\tclosing the other customer costs one stack.\n156\t\n157\t*In plain English.* Once every neighbour of a customer has an open stack, that\n158\tcustomer can close straight away without opening anything new. Doing so never\n159\tmakes later steps dearer, and it makes some cheaper, because one fewer stack\n160\tis left open. So a search that always closes such customers first loses\n161\tnothing. The only proviso is that the stacks already open must fit within the\n162\tbudget, since closing the free customers one by one costs exactly that many.\n163\t\n164\t### 4.2.3 Cost is narrowness, so the search decides pathwidth\n165\t\n166\tFor a full closing order $\\tau = c_1, \\dots, c_n$ from $\\emptyset$, write\n167\t$S_i = \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$ and\n168\t$\\partial X = \\{v \\notin X : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } X\\}$. The $i$-th step\n169\tcosts\n170\t$$|O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}| = 1 + |\\partial S_i|,$$\n171\tbecause $O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}$ is $c_i$ together with the customers\n172\toutside $S_i$ that have a neighbour in $S_i$. The maximum of these numbers over\n173\t$i$ is the *narrowness of the sequence* $\\tau$, in the dual shack process of\n174\tKornai & Tuza (1992), formalised as `Complex.outNarrowness`.\n175\t\n176\t**Theorem 4.3 (the search decides pathwidth and MOSP).** For nonempty $C$,\n177\t$$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\min_\\tau \\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) \\le k \\iff \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 \\le k.$$\n178\tFor a MOSP instance $M$ with at least one requirement, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$\n179\ton $G_M$ holds iff $Z(M) \\le k$.\n180\t\n181\t*Proof.* The first equivalence is Lemma 4.2 at the root. For the second, read\n182\t$\\tau$ backwards as a layout $L$, so that the cut after position $n - i$ of\n183\t$L$ separates $C \\setminus S_i$ (left) from $S_i$ (right). The left vertices\n184\twith a right neighbour are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So\n185\t$\\max_{1 \\le i < n} |\\partial S_i|$ is the vertex separation of $L$, and the\n186\tlast step ($i = n$, $\\partial C = \\emptyset$) costs 1. Hence\n187\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) = \\mathrm{vs}(L) + 1$. Minimising over $\\tau$ gives\n188\t$\\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$ by (E1). The MOSP form is (E2).\n189\t$\\square$ (The Lean statement counts the active suffix of $\\tau$ itself, the\n190\tdevelopment's convention, so no reversal appears there.) (Lean: `orderCost_ofFn_eq_outNarrowness`,\n191\t`orderCost_ofFn_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one`,\n192\t`searchSol_empty_iff_narrowness_le`,\n193\t`searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le`,\n194\t`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`; behind them\n195\t`Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` and\n196\t`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.)\n197\t\n198\tSo the only thing a refutation, $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$, may mean is\n199\t$Z(M) > k$, equivalently $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$. The rest of this section\n200\tshows that the pruning rules do not change the answer.\n201\t\n202\t*In plain English.* Close the customers one at a time. When a customer\n203\tcloses, every neighbour must already have its stack open, so the stacks open\n204\tat that moment are the customer itself plus every not-yet-closed customer\n205\ttouching the closed group. Read the closing order backwards and this is the\n206\tcount that vertex separation takes at each cut. So the best closing order uses\n207\texactly vertex separation plus one stacks, which is pathwidth plus one.\n208\t\n209\t**A modelling remark.** The code drops customers with no product. In $G_M$\n210\tthey are isolated vertices, which are free at the root and change nothing.\n211\tThis is argued, not formalised.\n212\t\n213\t---\n214\t\n215\t## 4.3 The rules\n216\t\n217\t### 4.3.0 What a rule must satisfy\n218\t\n219\tAt a node the search has a state $S$ with the invariant, and a set $Q$ of\n220\t*old moves* (section 4.3.5). Its *candidates* are the customers outside\n221\t$S \\cup Q$. The *cost cut* keeps the playable candidates $P$, in increasing\n222\tcustomer index. The cost cut is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\n223\tof $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. A *filter* then keeps a sublist $L \\subseteq P$, and the\n224\tsearch recurses on $S \\cdot c$ for $c \\in L$.\n225\t\n226\t**Node soundness.** Suppose $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$ for every\n227\t$q \\in Q$. The filter is node-sound at $(S, Q)$ if $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ implies\n228\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$ for some $c \\in L$ (Lean: `NodeSoundAt`;\n229\t`FilterSound` when it holds at every node with the invariant).\n230\t\n231\tEach rule is justified by a *covering*: a discarded move $r$ is covered by $c$\n232\twhen $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$. A node\n233\tis sound when every link a rule cites is a true covering and every chain of\n234\tcited links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$ (Lean:\n235\t`nodeSoundAt_of_covering`, with `Covered` for the chains). The second\n236\tcondition can fail even when every link is true, through a cycle. Section\n237\t4.3.4 has the example.\n238\t\n239\tThe rules run in the code's order: the free move on entry; the memo; the\n240\told-move candidate set; the cost cut; then the *definite move*, the *subset\n241\trule* and the *better move*, each once.\n242\t\n243\t### 4.3.1 The free move\n244\t\n245\t**Published form.** Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule. It is the\n246\t$\\mathrm{open} = 0$ case of their Theorem 1.\n247\t\n248\t**Precise form.** On entering a node at $T$, move to $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n249\t\n250\t**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\n251\tby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\n252\tvisits.\n253\t\n254\t### 4.3.2 The definite move\n255\t\n256\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\n257\tclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\n258\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n259\t|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4).\n260\t\n261\t**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n262\t$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\n263\twhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\n264\tpaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\n265\tand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\n266\tsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\n267\tkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\n268\tthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`).\n269\t\n270\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n271\t\n272\tWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n273\t\n274\t**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n275\t$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n276\t$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ holds iff $b(X) \\le b(S)$: the\n277\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\n278\t\n279\t*Proof.* For $d \\notin S$: $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$ iff\n280\t$N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup N[q] = O(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ iff $d \\in X$. For the second\n281\tclaim, $O(X) = O(S) \\cup N[q]$ gives\n282\t$b(X) = |O(S)| + \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - |X|$, while $b(S) = |O(S)| - |S|$, and\n283\t$|X| - |S| = \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `closeCount_eq`,\n284\t`isDefinite_iff`.)\n285\t\n286\t**Counterexample 4.5.** Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0, \\dots, 13$ with\n287\tthe 26 edges\n288\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n289\t&0\\text{–}3,\\ 0\\text{–}4,\\ 0\\text{–}7,\\ 0\\text{–}11,\\ 1\\text{–}2,\\ 1\\text{–}5,\\ 1\\text{–}6,\\ 2\\text{–}3,\\ 2\\text{–}4,\\ 5\\text{–}9,\\ 5\\text{–}11,\\ 5\\text{–}12,\\ 6\\text{–}8,\\\\\n290\t&6\\text{–}13,\\ 7\\text{–}9,\\ 7\\text{–}10,\\ 7\\text{–}13,\\ 8\\text{–}9,\\ 8\\text{–}10,\\ 8\\text{–}11,\\ 9\\text{–}10,\\ 9\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}12,\\ 10\\text{–}13,\\ 12\\text{–}13,\n291\t\\end{aligned}$$\n292\tand take $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 6$, $q = 0$. Then:\n293\t\n294\t- $S$ is a state, $O(S) = N[2] = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $b(S) = 3 \\le 6$;\n295\t- $o(0, S) = \\{0, 7, 11\\}$, so $\\mathrm{open}(0, S) = 3$, and\n296\t  $\\mathrm{cost}(S, 0) = |\\{0, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11\\}| = 6$, so $0$ is playable;\n297\t- the customers $d \\notin S$ with $o(d, S) \\subseteq \\{0, 7, 11\\}$ are $0$,\n298\t  $3$ and $4$ (both of the latter have $o = \\{0\\}$), so\n299\t  $\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge \\mathrm{open}(0, S)$;\n300\t- $0$ is the first customer in index order, so the code keeps $\\{0\\}$ alone;\n301\t- $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(S)$ holds: the order $1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$\n302\t  costs at most 6;\n303\t- the child is $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(X)$ fails: only seven\n304\t  sets are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none of them is\n305\t  $C$.\n306\t\n307\t![The counterexample to Theorem 1](figures/definite_counterexample.png)\n308\t\n309\t*Figure 4.1. The graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the state where the definite\n310\tmove fails. Customer 2 is closed, and 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\n311\tnew stacks 0, 7 and 11, and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule\n312\tkeeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them: closing\n313\tthem first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\n314\twith 0 cannot match. Regenerate with `python -m paper2.counterexample_figure`,\n315\twhich recomputes every labelled fact from the instance (PDF beside it).*\n316\t\n317\tSo Theorem 1 is false, both as published and as coded. (Lean:\n318\t`definiteMove_counterexample`, `cexGraph`, `cex_solvable`, and\n319\t`cexFamily_closed` for the seven sets, checked by `decide +kernel`.) The published implication itself is refuted as a universal statement, with\n320\tthe bridge from the free-closed child to $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ inside the proof,\n321\tunder the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\n322\talso counts closed customers and so is a weaker premise\n323\t(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n324\t`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`).\n325\t\n326\t**Why the published proof fails.** The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n327\tsolution $U' = S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [c_1, \\dots, c_m, q, \\dots]$ and claims that\n328\tafter each prefix \"we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\n329\tclose(q, S) extra stacks closed\" (PDF p. 6). Compare the two runs at a prefix\n330\t$T \\supseteq S$ of $U'$ that does not yet contain $q$. Moving $q$ forward\n331\tchanges the open stacks from $b(T)$ to $b(T \\cup X)$, and\n332\t$$b(T \\cup X) - b(T) = |N[q] \\setminus O(T)| - |X \\setminus T|.$$\n333\tAt $T = S$ this is $\\mathrm{open} - \\mathrm{close} \\le 0$. But the customers of\n334\t$X$ that $U'$ has *already* closed are in $T$, so they are no longer extra\n335\tstacks closed, while the stacks $q$ opens may still be new. In the example,\n336\t$U'$ may begin with $3$, after which $4$ is free and $T = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$. Then\n337\t$N[0] \\setminus O(T) = \\{7, 11\\}$ and $X \\setminus T = \\{0\\}$, so moving $0$\n338\tforward costs one more stack: $b(T) = 2$ and $b(T \\cup X) = 3$. Customers $3$\n339\tand $4$ each counted once in $\\mathrm{close}(0, S)$, but they share the single\n340\tnew stack $0$, and a solution that closes them first gains two closed stacks\n341\tfor one opened.\n342\t\n343\tThere is a second, independent slip. Even where the conclusion holds, the\n344\tliteral sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\n345\tbecause a customer that $q$ makes free stays open until its turn comes. With\n346\tedges $0\\text{–}3$ and $1\\text{–}2$, $S = \\emptyset$ and $q = 0$\n347\t($\\mathrm{open} = \\mathrm{close} = 2$), the order $1, 2, 0, 3$ costs 2 but\n348\t$0, 1, 2, 3$ costs 3. Closing $3$ right after $0$ fixes it. The argument needs\n349\tthe free moves.\n350\t\n351\t**Repair.** Call $q \\notin S$ *hereditarily definite* at $S$ when\n352\t$$b(X) \\le b(B) \\quad \\text{for every } B \\text{ with } S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X \\text{ and } q \\notin B.$$\n353\tBy Lemma 4.4, the code's premise is the case $B = S$. The repair asks that no\n354\tintermediate set avoiding $q$ is a better place to stand than $X$ (Lean:\n355\t`IsHereditarilyDefinite`; `IsHereditarilyDefinite.isDefinite`). In the\n356\tcounterexample it fails at $B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, where $b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X)$\n357\t(Lean: `not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex`).\n358\t\n359\t**Lemma 4.6 (open stacks are submodular).** For all $A, B \\subseteq C$,\n360\t$$b(A \\cup B) + b(A \\cap B) \\le b(A) + b(B).$$\n361\t\n362\t*Proof.* Since $T \\subseteq O(T)$, $b(T) = |O(T)| - |T|$. Now\n363\t$O(A \\cup B) = O(A) \\cup O(B)$ and $O(A \\cap B) \\subseteq O(A) \\cap O(B)$, so\n364\t$|O(A \\cup B)| + |O(A \\cap B)| \\le |O(A)| + |O(B)|$. Subtract\n365\t$|A \\cup B| + |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B|$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n366\t`openStacks_submodular`, from `openStacks_add_card`.)\n367\t\n368\t*In plain English.* Count the stacks opened by two groups of closed customers.\n369\tThe union of the groups opens exactly what either opens, and their overlap\n370\topens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall\n371\tshort of the two on the right, and subtracting the customers themselves, who\n372\tbalance exactly, keeps that.\n373\t\n374\t**Theorem 4.7 (the repaired definite move is sound).** If $q \\notin S$ is\n375\thereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(X)$, with no other\n376\thypothesis. At a state with the invariant,\n377\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, so keeping only a\n378\tplayable hereditarily definite $q$ is sound.\n379\t\n380\t*Proof.* Take a closing order $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ from $S$ of cost at most $k$,\n381\twith prefixes $T_i = S \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$, and let $c_j = q$. Build an\n382\torder from $X$ by following $c_1, \\dots, c_{j-1}$ and skipping any customer\n383\talready in $X$. For $i < j$ with $c_i \\notin X$, the step closes $c_i$ at\n384\t$T_{i-1} \\cup X$ and costs $b(T_i \\cup X) + 1$. Put $Z = T_i$. Then\n385\t$S \\subseteq Z \\cap X \\subseteq X$ and $q \\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n386\t$b(X) \\le b(Z \\cap X)$, and Lemma 4.6 gives\n387\t$$b(Z \\cup X) \\le b(Z) + b(X) - b(Z \\cap X) \\le b(Z).$$\n388\tSo the step costs at most $b(T_i) + 1 = \\mathrm{cost}(T_{i-1}, c_i) \\le k$.\n389\tAfter these steps the closed set is $T_{j-1} \\cup X$. It contains $T_j$, and\n390\tsince $S \\subseteq T_{j-1}$ its opened set is $O(T_{j-1}) \\cup N[q] = O(T_j)$.\n391\tThe rest of the original order gives $P_k(T_j)$, so Lemma 4.1 gives\n392\t$P_k(T_{j-1} \\cup X)$, and together $P_k(X)$.\n393\t\n394\tFor the search's form, the invariant and Lemma 4.2 give $P_k(S)$, hence\n395\t$P_k(X)$, hence $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(X)) = \\mathrm{Sol}_k(X)$ by Lemma 4.2\n396\tagain. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`, by\n397\t`solvable_union_cl_of_hereditarilyDefinite` and `stepCost_union_cl_le`;\n398\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`.)\n399\t\n400\t*In plain English.* Take any good closing order and imagine closing $q$, with\n401\teverything it makes free, before anything else. At each moment before the\n402\torder reaches $q$, compare the two situations. The hereditary condition says\n403\tthat standing on the \"$q$ and its free customers\" side never leaves more stacks\n404\topen than standing at any point between where you started and there, provided\n405\t$q$ itself has not closed. Submodularity turns that into a step-by-step\n406\tguarantee: the order with $q$ first never has more stacks open than the\n407\toriginal. Once the original order closes $q$, the two have the same stacks\n408\topen and the shifted one has closed at least as much.\n409\t\n410\t**Corollary.** Every $q$ with $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$ is hereditarily\n411\tdefinite, so the code's rule is right in those cases. For\n412\t$S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X$ with $q \\notin B$, the stacks in $O(X)$ but not in\n413\t$O(B)$ lie in $N[q] \\setminus O(S) = o(q, S)$, so there is at most one of\n414\tthem. Since $O(B) \\subseteq O(X)$, $b(X) - b(B) = |O(X) \\setminus O(B)| -\n415\t|X \\setminus B| \\le 1 - 1 = 0$, because $q \\in X \\setminus B$. (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one`.)\n416\t\n417\t**The matching form.** The repair quantifies over all sets $B$. An equivalent\n418\tform is cheap. Let $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$, the customers the child\n419\tcloses besides $q$, and for $D \\subseteq Y$ let\n420\t$o(D) = \\bigcup_{d \\in D} o(d, S)$. Every $d \\in Y$ has\n421\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$, so consider the bipartite graph joining each\n422\t$d \\in Y$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n423\t\n424\t**Theorem 4.8 (the repair is a matching condition).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n425\t$q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff some set $M \\subseteq Y$ with\n426\t$|M| \\ge \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ can be matched injectively to stacks\n427\t$f(d) \\in o(d, S)$. Equivalently, the largest matching of the bipartite graph\n428\thas at least $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ edges.\n429\t\n430\t*Proof.* Let $q \\notin S$; if $q \\in S$ then $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) = 0$ and both\n431\tsides hold trivially. The sets $B$ of the repair are exactly $B = S \\cup D$\n432\twith $D \\subseteq Y$. Since $|O(S \\cup D)| = |O(S)| + |o(D)|$ and\n433\t$|X| = |S| + 1 + |Y|$,\n434\t$$b(X) \\le b(S \\cup D) \\iff \\mathrm{open}(q, S) + |D| \\le |o(D)| + |Y| + 1 \\iff |D| - |o(D)| \\le \\delta,$$\n435\twith $\\delta = |Y| + 1 - \\mathrm{open}(q, S)$. (At $D = \\emptyset$ this gives\n436\t$\\delta \\ge 0$.) By the deficiency form of Hall's theorem (Ore 1955), the\n437\tlargest matching of $Y$ into the stacks has size\n438\t$|Y| - \\max_{D \\subseteq Y} (|D| - |o(D)|)$. So the repair holds iff that\n439\tmaximum is at most $\\delta$, iff the largest matching has size at least\n440\t$|Y| - \\delta = \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. $\\square$\n441\t\n442\tThe Lean proof uses the textbook reduction of the deficiency form to Hall's\n443\ttheorem: give every $d \\in Y$ the same $\\delta$ dummy stacks besides $o(d, S)$,\n444\tcheck Hall's condition from the deficiency inequality, apply Mathlib's\n445\t`Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_existsInjective'`, and keep the customers\n446\tmatched to real stacks, of which at most $\\delta$ are lost. (Lean:\n447\t`HasDefiniteMatching`, `card_opened_union_eq`, `card_cl_insert`,\n448\t`hall_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`, `exists_matching_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`,\n449\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_of_matching`, and\n450\t`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.)\n451\t\n452\t*In plain English.* Closing $q$ opens some new stacks, and it lets some other\n453\tcustomers close for free, each of whom needed one or more of those new stacks.\n454\tThe rule is safe exactly when you can pair all but one of the new stacks with\n455\ta different free customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing $0$\n456\topens three new stacks, $0$, $7$ and $11$, and frees customers $3$ and $4$.\n457\tBoth of them needed only stack $0$, so only one pairing is possible where two\n458\tare required.\n459\t\n460\t### 4.3.3 The subset rule\n461\t\n462\t**Published form.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can\n463\talways play i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will\n464\tclose i in any case. Hence move j can be removed from the candidates R\n465\tconsidered for the next customer to close\" (§2, PDF p. 5).\n466\t\n467\t**Precise form, as coded.** Say $d$ *dominates* $r$ at $S$ when $d \\ne r$,\n468\t$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, and either the inclusion is strict or $d < r$.\n469\tThe rule drops every $r \\in P$ dominated by some $d \\in C \\setminus S$, where\n470\t$d$ ranges over all remaining customers, including those in $Q$. If every\n471\tcandidate would be dropped, it keeps $P$ whole (the *fallback*). It runs only\n472\twhen the definite move did not fire (Lean: `Dominates`, `IsSubsetDominated`,\n473\t`subsetFilter`).\n474\t\n475\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$\n476\tfor the dominator $d$. **Sound as coded.**\n477\t\n478\t**Lemma 4.9 (the covering).** If $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$ and\n479\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, then\n480\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$, for every set\n481\t$S$, with no invariant and no tie-break. Moreover $d$ is playable.\n482\t\n483\t*Proof.* $N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup o(d, S) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, so\n484\t$d \\in S \\cdot r$, and $O(S \\cup \\{d\\}) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ gives\n485\t$S \\cdot d \\subseteq S \\cdot r$ and $\\mathrm{cost}(S, d) \\le \\mathrm{cost}(S, r)$.\n486\tIf $r \\in S \\cdot d$, the two opened sets are equal and $S \\cdot d = S \\cdot r$.\n487\tOtherwise close $r$ at $S \\cdot d$. It costs\n488\t$|O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot d| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S| =\n489\t\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, and it lands on\n490\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S \\cup \\{r\\})) = S \\cdot r$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n491\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, with\n492\t`mem_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n493\t`cl_insert_subset_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n494\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n495\t`stepCost_le_of_newlyOpened_subset`.)\n496\t\n497\t**Proposition 4.10 (node soundness).** At any state $S \\ne C$, if every\n498\t$q \\in Q$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, the subset filter is node-sound.\n499\tWhen a solution exists, the fallback never fires.\n500\t\n501\t*Proof.* Domination is irreflexive and transitive, so it is a strict order.\n502\tGiven $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, some playable $r$ has $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r)$,\n503\tand $r \\notin Q$. Among the customers $m \\notin S$ with\n504\t$o(m, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, take the least by $(|o(m, S)|, m)$. Any dominator\n505\tof $m$ would come earlier in that order, so $m$ is undominated. By Lemma 4.9,\n506\t$m$ is playable and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot m)$ holds, so $m \\notin Q$. So $m$\n507\tis a candidate that survives. $\\square$ (Lean: `not_dominates_self`,\n508\t`Dominates.trans`, `exists_undominated`, `subsetKept_nonempty`,\n509\t`subsetFilter_sound`.)\n510\t\n511\t*In plain English.* If closing $d$ opens only stacks that closing $r$ would\n512\topen anyway, then closing $r$ makes $d$ free. So closing $d$ first, then $r$,\n513\treaches the same place as closing $r$, never at a higher cost, and the search\n514\tmay as well try $d$. Follow such domination downwards and you reach a customer\n515\tnobody dominates, which the rule keeps.\n516\t\n517\t**The tie-break is necessary (Counterexample 4.11).** Without it, two\n518\tcustomers with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go. Take the\n519\t7-customer graph with edges $0\\text{–}2$, $0\\text{–}3$, $0\\text{–}6$,\n520\t$1\\text{–}4$, $1\\text{–}6$, $3\\text{–}6$, $4\\text{–}5$, $4\\text{–}6$, the\n521\tstate $S = \\{2\\}$ and $k = 3$. The playable candidates are $\\{0, 3, 5\\}$, with\n522\t$o(0, S) = o(3, S) = \\{3, 6\\}$ and $o(5, S) = \\{4, 5\\}$. Without the tie-break\n523\t$0$ and $3$ both go, $5$ survives, so the fallback does not fire, and the\n524\tfilter keeps $\\{5\\}$. A solution exists from $S$ ($0, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6$), but none\n525\tfrom $S \\cdot 5 = \\{2, 5\\}$, where no move costs at most 3. With the tie-break,\n526\tonly $3$ goes and the filter keeps $\\{0, 5\\}$. (Lean:\n527\t`noTieBreak_counterexample`, `tieGraph`, `WeakDominates`, `weakSubsetFilter`.)\n528\tThe paper's own form, which requires $d < r$ even for a strict inclusion, is a\n529\tsubrelation of the code's, uses the same covering and is acyclic by index. It\n530\tis checked, not stated in Lean.\n531\t\n532\t### 4.3.4 The better move\n533\t\n534\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 2.** Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are\n535\tplayable and close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is\n536\ta solution there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.2, PDF p. 6). The\n537\tproof begins: \"The conditions imply that if r is played now, q becomes a\n538\tdefinite move.\"\n539\t\n540\t**Precise form, as coded** (the C only, since 2026-09-26). The input is the\n541\tlist $W$ of subset survivors, in index order. Let $X_r = O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. A\n542\tmember $r$ is dropped if some earlier $q \\in W$, among the first $L$ of $W$ if\n543\t$L > 0$, satisfies\n544\t\n545\t- *(premise 3)* $|(X_r \\cup N[q]) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k$, and\n546\t- *(premise 4)* $\\mathrm{open}' \\le \\mathrm{close}'$, where\n547\t  $\\mathrm{open}' = |N[q] \\setminus X_r|$ and $\\mathrm{close}'$ counts the\n548\t  $d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus X_r \\subseteq N[q] \\setminus X_r$.\n549\t\n550\tPremise 2 is $q \\in W \\subseteq P$. Whether $q$ itself survives is not\n551\tconsulted. (Lean: `IsBetter`, `betterCite`, `withinLimit`, `betterFilterBy`.)\n552\t\n553\t**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n554\t\n555\t**Lemma 4.12 (the premise is Theorem 1 at the child).** Premises 3 and 4 hold\n556\tiff $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q) \\le k$ and the code's definite premise\n557\tholds for $q$ at the child $T = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$.\n558\t\n559\t*Proof.* Premise 3 is $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q)$ by definition. Since\n560\t$O(T) = X_r$, $\\mathrm{open}' = \\mathrm{open}(q, T)$. A customer\n561\t$d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ has $N[d] \\setminus X_r \\ne \\emptyset$ exactly when\n562\t$d \\notin T$, and then $N[d] \\setminus X_r = o(d, T)$. So\n563\t$\\mathrm{close}' = \\mathrm{close}(q, T)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isBetter_iff`,\n564\t`betterOpen_eq`, `betterClose_eq`.)\n565\t\n566\tSo the better move is Theorem 1 at the child, followed by a swap of $q$ and\n567\t$r$. The swap is sound. The appeal to Theorem 1 is not.\n568\t\n569\t**Counterexample 4.13.** Take the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the root,\n570\t$S = \\emptyset$, with $k = 6$ and $Q = \\emptyset$. No playable customer meets\n571\tthe definite premise, so the definite move does not fire. Customers $0$ and $2$\n572\tboth survive the subset rule, $0 < 2$, and premises 3 and 4 hold for $r = 2$,\n573\t$q = 0$, so the code drops $2$ citing $0$. But $S \\cdot 2 = \\{2\\}$ has a\n574\tsolution (Counterexample 4.5), and $S \\cdot 0 = \\{0\\}$ has none. The child\n575\t$\\{2\\}$ is exactly the state at which Theorem 1 wrongly moves $0$ forward.\n576\t(Lean: `betterMove_counterexample`, by an invariant family of 18 sets,\n577\t`bmFamily_closed`.) Theorem 2's published implication is refuted the same way, under both\n578\treadings of $\\mathrm{close}$ (`chuStuckey_theorem2_false`,\n579\t`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\n580\ta solution, so this is a *false link*, not a lost node (Lean:\n581\t`betterMove_counterexample_node`). No node where the better move loses the last\n582\tsolution has been found (section 4.5).\n583\t\n584\t**Repair.** Cite $q$ for $r$ only if premise 3 holds and $q$ is hereditarily\n585\tdefinite at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ (Lean: `IsRepairedBetter`;\n586\t`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n587\t4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n588\t\n589\t**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,\n590\t$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$ and the repaired premise holds, then\n591\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n592\t\n593\t*Proof.* Let $T = S \\cdot r$, which has the invariant because $r$ is playable.\n594\tIf $q \\in T$, put $T' = T$. Otherwise Theorem 4.7 at $T$ gives\n595\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(T')$ for $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T \\cup \\{q\\})$. Now let\n596\t$Z = S \\cdot q$. Both $\\mathrm{cl}(Z \\cup \\{r\\})$ and $T'$ equal\n597\t$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[r])$. If $r \\in Z$ then $Z = T'$. Otherwise\n598\tclosing $r$ at $Z$ costs\n599\t$$|O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus Z| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| = |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k,$$\n600\twhere the middle equality exchanges $q$ and $r$, both of which are opened and\n601\tneither of which is in $S$, and the last step is premise 3. So $r$ is a\n602\tplayable move from $Z$ to $T'$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(Z)$ follows. $\\square$\n603\t(Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n604\t`searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`, with `card_sdiff_insert_eq`.) The\n605\timplication needs $r$ playable, not $q$. Playability of $q$ is what makes $q$ a\n606\tchild of the search.\n607\t\n608\t*In plain English.* The better move says: \"if closing $r$ now would make $q$ a\n609\tsafe next move, then closing $q$ first and $r$ second is at least as good as\n610\tclosing $r$ first.\" The swap at the end is fine, because both orders end in the\n611\tsame place and neither step overflows. What fails is \"would make $q$ a safe\n612\tnext move\", which was Theorem 1. With the repaired test there, the argument\n613\tgoes through unchanged.\n614\t\n615\t**Two earlier faults, now proved.** The 2026-09-26 fix corrected two bugs in\n616\tthis rule, both caught by testing (`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §7). Lean\n617\tnow proves a counterexample to each.\n618\t\n619\t- *Bug A, the close count.* Counting also the customers that $r$ finishes on\n620\t  its own makes the rule's own conclusion false, on a 12-customer graph at\n621\t  $k = 4$ (Lean: `IsBetterOld`, `bugA_counterexample`, `bugAGraph`).\n622\t- *Bug B, the order of the rules.* Running the better move before the subset\n623\t  rule lets the subset rule cite a customer the better move has already\n624\t  dropped. On an 8-customer graph at $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 4$, the better move drops\n625\t  $3$ citing $0$ and the subset rule drops $0$ citing $3$. Every premise is the\n626\t  corrected one and true, and the node is lost through the cycle (Lean:\n627\t  `oldOrderFilter`, `bugB_counterexample`, `bugBGraph`).\n628\t\n629\t### 4.3.5 The old move\n630\t\n631\t**Published form.** \"**Theorem 3.** Let S = [s₁, s₂, …, sₙ]. Suppose that\n632\tS′ = [s₁, s₂, .., sₘ, q, sₘ₊₁, …, sₙ] is playable, then if U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is\n633\ta solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (§3.3, PDF p. 7), with the rule\n634\t\"if it is found that at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and\n635\tU is playable, then q can immediately be pruned\".\n636\t\n637\t**Precise form, as coded.** A node carries a set $Q$ of old moves, empty at the\n638\troot. When a candidate's subtree returns *false* without aborting, the\n639\tcandidate joins the node's list `seen`. A later sibling $c'$ passes to its child\n640\tthose $q \\in$ `seen` with\n641\t$$\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c') = |(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c']) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k,$$\n642\twhich is the test that the step $c'$ stays playable with $q$ reinserted before\n643\tit. Old moves are removed from the candidates (Lean: `Inherits`, `pathEnd`).\n644\t\n645\t**Claimed soundness.** Every $q \\in Q$ at a node has\n646\t$\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. **Sound as coded.**\n647\t\n648\t**Lemma 4.15 (reinsertion).** If $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c) \\le k$, then\n649\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n650\tfor every set $S$, with no invariant. Iterated along a path whose every move\n651\tpasses the test: if the $q$ branch at an ancestor has no solution, neither has\n652\t$q$ at the end of the path.\n653\t\n654\t*Proof.* Both $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)$ and\n655\t$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{c\\} \\cup S \\cdot q)$ equal $\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c])$.\n656\tIf $c \\in S \\cdot q$, then the second is $S \\cdot q$ itself. Otherwise $c$ is a\n657\tmove at $S \\cdot q$ of cost\n658\t$|O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot q| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k$,\n659\tbecause $S \\cup \\{q\\} \\subseteq S \\cdot q$. Induction on the path gives the\n660\tsecond statement. $\\square$ (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`,\n661\t`cl_insert_cl_insert_comm`, `stepCost_cl_le`, `searchSol_reinsert_path`,\n662\t`not_searchSol_of_oldMove`.)\n663\t\n664\t**The test is necessary.** On the path $4\\text{–}0\\text{–}2\\text{–}1\\text{–}3$\n665\twith $S = \\emptyset$, $q = 2$, $c = 3$ and $k = 2$, the move $c$ is playable but\n666\t$\\mathrm{cost}(\\{2\\}, 3) = 3$. Here\n667\t$\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{2\\} \\cup \\mathrm{cl}\\{3\\})) = \\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{1, 2, 3\\})$\n668\tholds and $\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{2\\})$ does not. This is the smallest such graph,\n669\tby exhaustion to 5 vertices (Lean: `reinsert_needs_test`). Chu & Stuckey make\n670\tthe same point: the condition \"is in fact crucial\" (PDF p. 7).\n671\t\n672\t*In plain English.* Suppose that at some earlier node the search tried closing\n673\t$q$ and found no way to finish. Since then it has closed some other customers.\n674\tIf, at every one of those steps, closing $q$ first would still have kept the\n675\tstep within budget, then \"close $q$ now\" leads to a place the search could also\n676\thave reached from the earlier attempt. That attempt failed, so this one fails\n677\ttoo, and $q$ can be skipped.\n678\t\n679\tThe paper's \"synergy\" (adding $r$ to $Q$ when an old move is better than $r$)\n680\tis implemented in neither the C nor the Python and is not covered.\n681\t\n682\t### 4.3.6 The memo\n683\t\n684\t**Published form.** Nogood recording: \"if it failed, we record the nogood and\n685\treturn false when we revisit it\" (PDF p. 4).\n686\t\n687\t**Precise form, as coded.** When a node's loop finishes with no child answering\n688\t*true* and without an abort, its closed set $S$ is recorded. At any later node\n689\twith the same closed set the search answers *false* at once. The memo may\n690\tstore fewer entries (a limit, a load factor), which only stores less.\n691\t\n692\t**Claimed soundness.** A recorded $S$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n693\t\n694\t**Lemma 4.16 (the key).** If $b(T) \\le k$, $b(T') \\le k$ and\n695\t$\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{cl}(T')$, then $P_k(T) \\iff P_k(T')$. So a state\n696\trefuted once is refuted whatever path reached it.\n697\t\n698\t*Proof.* Lemma 4.2 at both sides. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n699\t\n700\tThe memo is sound once every recorded entry is a genuine refutation. With old\n701\tmove on, that is not a property of the node alone, and it is proved for the\n702\twhole run in section 4.4.2.\n703\t\n704\t---\n705\t\n706\t## 4.4 The composition and the theorem\n707\t\n708\t### 4.4.1 The filter\n709\t\n710\tThe filter runs in the order\n711\t\n712\t$$\\text{definite move} \\;\\to\\; \\text{subset rule} \\;\\to\\; \\text{better move},$$\n713\t\n714\teach once, and each cites only customers still standing or in $Q$. If the\n715\tdefinite move fires, $L = [q]$. Otherwise the subset rule runs over $P$ with\n716\tdominators from all of $C \\setminus S$, while nothing has yet been discarded.\n717\tThe better move then runs over the subset survivors and cites only an earlier\n718\tsurvivor (Lean: `fullFilter`; `codeFullFilter` with the code's premises,\n719\t`repairedFullFilter` with both repairs).\n720\t\n721\t**Proposition 4.17 (the repaired filter is node-sound).** At every state with\n722\tthe invariant, for every limit $L$ and every set $Q$ of refuted old moves,\n723\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$ with both repairs is\n724\tnode-sound.\n725\t\n726\t*Proof.* If the definite move fires on a playable hereditarily definite $q$,\n727\tTheorem 4.7 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Otherwise Proposition 4.10 gives\n728\ta subset survivor with a solution. Let $w$ be the earliest such survivor in\n729\tindex order. If the better move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor $q$\n730\tunder the repaired premise, and Theorem 4.14 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\n731\twhich contradicts the choice of $w$. So $w \\in L$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n732\t`betterFilterBy_sound`, `fullFilter_sound`, `repairedFilter_sound`,\n733\t`repairedFullFilter_sound`.)\n734\t\n735\tThe argument needs no chain of citations and does not use $L$. Bug B shows\n736\tthat the order matters. With the better move first, a subset link can point\n737\tbackwards at a customer already discarded, and every premise can be true while\n738\tthe chain cycles.\n739\t\n740\t*In plain English.* If the safe-move test fires, the one move it keeps is\n741\tproved safe. If it does not, the subset rule always keeps some move that still\n742\tleads to a solution. Among the moves that do, look at the first one. The better\n743\tmove can only throw it out by pointing at an even earlier move that it proves\n744\tat least as good, and there is none. So something that still works always\n745\tsurvives.\n746\t\n747\t### 4.4.2 Runs, the memo and old move together\n748\t\n749\tA run of the search is described by a big-step semantics of its *false*\n750\tanswers (Lean: `Exec`). A node intersects $Q$ with its remaining customers,\n751\tenumerates the filter's output in any order, runs each child with any subset of\n752\tthe old moves that pass the reinsertion test, adds each refuted child to\n753\t`seen`, and records its state in the memo or not. A memo hit answers *false*.\n754\tAn aborted run produces no `Exec` at all, which matches the code: an abort\n755\tanswers *unknown* and records nothing. So the child order, the memo policy and\n756\told move on or off are all covered.\n757\t\n758\t**Theorem 4.18 (runs are sound).** Let the filter be node-sound and return only\n759\tplayable customers. Suppose that at the start of a run the invariant holds at\n760\t$S$, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every $q \\in Q \\setminus S$\n761\thas $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Then the run leaves only genuine memo\n762\tentries, and if it answers *false* then $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n763\t\n764\t*Proof.* Induct on the run, which follows the order in which subtrees complete.\n765\tEach entry of `seen` at a node is either a sibling subtree that has already\n766\tcompleted with *false*, refuted by the induction hypothesis, or one of the\n767\tnode's own old moves, refuted by hypothesis. Each old move a child inherits\n768\tpassed the test, so Lemma 4.15 makes it genuinely refuted at the child. When a\n769\tnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness\n770\tgives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, so recording $S$ is correct. A memo hit is\n771\tcorrect by the hypothesis on the memo. $\\square$ (Lean: `Exec.sound`,\n772\t`exec_root_sound`; `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` with\n773\t`repairedFullFilter_filterSound` and `repairedFullFilter_playable`.)\n774\t\n775\t**Why one implementation refused the combination.** The Python reference drops\n776\tthe memo under old move, on the ground that a failure reached with old-move\n777\tpruning depends on the branches an ancestor searched. What is true in this is\n778\tlocal: a node's run *taken on its own*, with an old move that is not refuted,\n779\tcan refute a solvable state and record it. One isolated customer with $k = 1$\n780\tand the old move $Q = \\{0\\}$ is enough (Lean: `exec_fake_oldMove`). But the\n781\thypothesis of Theorem 4.18 holds in every real run, because every old move is a\n782\trefutation completed earlier in the same run. So the combination is sound,\n783\twhich is what the C has always run and what every production refutation used.\n784\tWhat is lost is that a memo entry cannot be checked without re-deriving the\n785\tcited subtree's $Q$, which is why the certificate checker declines that\n786\tconfiguration.\n787\t\n788\t### 4.4.3 The theorem\n789\t\n790\t**Theorem 4.19 (the revised search is sound).** Run the customer search with\n791\tfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter\n792\t$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$, with the definite move\n793\tfiring only on a hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only\n794\tunder the repaired premise, with any limit $L$, any child order and any memo\n795\tpolicy. If the run refutes $k$ from the root, then:\n796\t\n797\t| conclusion | Lean |\n798\t|---|---|\n799\t| no closing order costs at most $k$, $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` |\n800\t| $k < \\nu(G)$, the narrowness | `exec_repairedFullFilter_narrowness` |\n801\t| $\\mathrm{pw}(G) > k - 1$, for nonempty $C$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth` |\n802\t| on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a requirement, $Z(M) > k$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` |\n803\t| the same in pathwidth form, $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospGraph_pathwidth` |\n804\t\n805\t*Proof.* Proposition 4.17 makes the filter node-sound, and it returns only\n806\tplayable customers. At the root the invariant holds, the memo is empty and\n807\t$Q = \\emptyset$, so Theorem 4.18 gives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$. Lemma 4.2\n808\tat the root turns this into $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$, and Theorem 4.3 turns it into\n809\t$\\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 > k$ and, on $G_M$, $Z(M) > k$. $\\square$\n810\t\n811\t*In plain English.* Each rule throws away only moves that some kept move is\n812\tproved to be at least as good as. The memo and the old-move list only ever\n813\tremember failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when the\n814\tsearch says \"no\", every way of finishing was either tried or provably no better\n815\tthan one that was tried, and no closing order fits in $k$ stacks. By the\n816\tearlier sections, that means the instance needs more than $k$ stacks and its\n817\tgraph has pathwidth at least $k$.\n818\t\n819\t### 4.4.4 What holds for the code as it stands\n820\t\n821\tThe code runs the unrepaired premises: $\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ for\n822\tthe definite move, and premises 3 and 4 for the better move. Its filter is\n823\t**not** node-sound. On the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at $k = 6$, for every\n824\t$L$, it keeps only $0$ at the state $\\{2\\}$, which has a solution, while\n825\t$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{0, 2\\})$ has none (Lean: `codeFullFilter_cex`). Against the named\n826\tproperty `CodeFilterSound` (the code's filter is node-sound at every state of\n827\tone instance at one $k$), this is `not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph`.\n828\t\n829\tWhat holds is conditional, and the condition is named rather than assumed.\n830\t\n831\t- **The gap, named.** `CodeRunSound` says that the code's run expands only\n832\t  nodes at which its filter keeps a solution whenever one exists. It is a\n833\t  hypothesis, not a theorem. Under it, a code refutation is genuine and gives\n834\t  $Z(M) > k$ (Lean: `ExecOn`, `ExecOn.sound`, `codeExec_sound_of_runSound`,\n835\t  `codeExec_pathwidth_of_runSound`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_runSound`).\n836\t- **A checkable condition at one node.** `CodeNodeRepaired` holds at a node\n837\t  when, if the definite move fires, its pick is hereditarily definite, and, if\n838\t  it does not fire, every customer the better move drops has *some* earlier\n839\t  cited survivor meeting the repaired premise. It implies node soundness\n840\t  (Lean: `nodeSoundAt_codeFullFilter_of_repaired`). A run of the code whose\n841\t  every expanded node passes it is sound and gives $Z(M) > k$ (Lean:\n842\t  `codeExec_sound_of_repaired`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`). This is what\n843\t  a certificate would have to carry to certify a refutation by the unrepaired\n844\t  code.\n845\t\n846\t**Why a false refutation is hard to produce.** Every node the search expands is\n847\treached from the root by playable moves. If the root has no solution, no\n848\texpanded node has one, and every expanded node is node-sound for free. So a\n849\tfalse refutation needs a $k$ at which a solution exists, together with a lost\n850\tnode on every branch that leads to a solution.\n851\t\n852\t**Lost nodes do occur in real runs.** In the check of section 4.5, the code's\n853\town run reaches a node where its filter loses the last solution in 58 of\n854\t570,206 runs. All 58 are on graphs built around the definite-move\n855\tcounterexample (29 graphs, each at two values of $L$), all at\n856\t$k = \\mathrm{opt}$ and never at $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$, as the previous paragraph\n857\trequires. The smallest has 14 customers. Every one of the 58 runs found a\n858\tsolution through another branch and answered correctly. In 26 of them the lost\n859\tsubtree joined `seen` and passed a non-refuted old move to its siblings, at 83\n860\tnodes in all, which is the cascade Theorem 4.18's hypothesis excludes. It did\n861\tnot change an answer. Over the same runs, `CodeNodeRepaired` held at\n862\t1,781,000 of 1,782,100 expanded nodes and failed at 1,100, most of which still\n863\tkept a solution.\n864\t\n865\t**The certificate checker.** The search certificate\n866\t(`learning/search_certificate.py`) checks each pruning step against a premise.\n867\tFor the free moves, the memo, the subset rule, the covering condition and the\n868\told moves, the premise it checks is the one the Lean lemma needs. For the\n869\tdefinite and better steps it checks the code's premises, which\n870\tCounterexamples 4.5 and 4.13 show can certify a false link. A certificate whose\n871\tdefinite and better steps meet the repaired premises proves $Z(M) > k$ with no\n872\ttrust in the search, by `nodeSoundAt_of_covering` at each node, then\n873\t`ExecOn.sound`, then Theorem 4.19's chain. That the checker's Python computes\n874\tthe quantities named is a reading of the code, backed by tests, and not proved.\n875\t\n876\t**Open.** Whether the unrepaired code ever answers *unsat* wrongly on a whole\n877\tinstance. None is known.\n878\t\n879\t---\n880\t\n881\t## 4.5 The evidence\n882\t\n883\tEvery statement above was checked by brute force before it was proved.\n884\t`paper2/search_check.py` implements the model and the rules from the written\n885\tstatements, sharing no code with the solver, and compares them with an exact\n886\toracle for $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$ computed over all states. The first check (item 06)\n887\tcovered every labelled graph on 1 to 6 vertices (33,867 graphs), every graph on\n888\t7 vertices under the identity and 24 random labellings (26,100), 6,000 random\n889\tgraphs on 8 to 16 vertices, and three pinned instances, at every $k$. For the\n890\trules as coded it found zero failures of node soundness, of cited links, of the\n891\tcovering condition and of whole-search answers, over 111,288,536, 90,915,044\n892\tand 355,181,036 node checks in the three families. It also confirmed that both\n893\tpre-fix forms of the better move fail: Bug A's conclusion first fails at 12\n894\tvertices, and Bug B first loses a node at 8. The same runs went through the C\n895\tsolver, and **the port matched the C in answer and node count on all 4,418,084\n896\truns**, including the faulty variants. That is what shows the rules of section\n897\t4.3 are the rules the C runs.\n898\t\n899\tThat check did not see Theorem 1's fault. The smallest graph on which the\n900\tdefinite move loses the last solution has 14 customers. There is none on any\n901\tgraph to 9 vertices (exhaustive) and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10\n902\tvertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was found by a hunt in a\n903\t\"gadget\" family built from the failed proof, and minimised by deletion to 14\n904\tcustomers and 26 edges, never fewer. Under the repair and its matching form the\n905\thunt found zero losses in 2.05 G premise checks. A port of the search with the\n906\tcode's definite move gave no false refutation at the optimum on 600,000 random\n907\tgraphs at 10 to 18 vertices and 90,000 gadget graphs at 12 to 17. The final\n908\tcheck (item 12) ran 570,206 searches with memo and old move: the repaired\n909\tsearch agreed with the oracle on every one, and the code gave zero wrong\n910\tanswers. Each later item has its own check table in `search_soundness.md`\n911\t§4.1.4 to §5.4, all with zero failures for the proved statements.\n912\t\n913\t---\n914\t\n915\t## 4.6 What changes in practice\n916\t\n917\t### 4.6.1 The revised algorithm\n918\t\n919\tThe search is unchanged except for two tests. Below, $\\mathrm{MaxMatch}(S, q)$\n920\tis the size of a largest matching between the customers\n921\t$Y = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the stacks\n922\t$o(q, S)$, joining $d$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n923\t\n924\t```\n925\tfilter(S, Q):                                   # S a state, Q the old moves\n926\t  P := [c not in S ∪ Q : cost(S, c) ≤ k], in index order\n927\t  for q in P:                                   # definite move\n928\t    if open(q, S) ≤ close(q, S)                 #   the published test\n929\t       and MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1:     #   the repair (Theorem 4.8)\n930\t      return [q]\n931\t  W := [r in P : no d in C∖S dominates r]       # subset rule, index tie-break\n932\t  if W is empty: W := P                         #   fallback\n933\t  kept := []\n934\t  for r in W, in index order:                   # better move\n935\t    T := cl(S ∪ {r})\n936\t    drop := exists an earlier q in W (among the first L if L > 0) with\n937\t              |(O(T) ∪ N[q]) ∖ (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k   #   premise 3\n938\t              and open(q, T) ≤ close(q, T)      #   premise 4, Theorem 2's test\n939\t              and MaxMatch(T, q) ≥ open(q, T) − 1   # the repair at the child\n940\t    if not drop: append r to kept\n941\t  return kept\n942\t```\n943\t\n944\tThe free moves, the memo, the old moves with their reinsertion test, the cost\n945\tcut and the child order are as before. Keeping the published test in front of\n946\tthe matching is only a shortcut, since the repair implies it.\n947\t\n948\t### 4.6.2 Its cost\n949\t\n950\tThe repair adds one bipartite matching per candidate that passes the published\n951\ttest, and nothing for the others. The matching is small: it joins at most\n952\t$\\mathrm{close} - 1$ customers to $\\mathrm{open}$ stacks, and it can stop as\n953\tsoon as it has $\\mathrm{open} - 1$ edges. Every candidate with\n954\t$\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ passes at once (Corollary to Theorem 4.7). In the better\n955\tmove the same matching is computed at the child $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$,\n956\tonce per pair $(r, q)$ that passes premises 3 and 4. By Theorem 4.8 the test is\n957\texact: where it fails, the repaired premise fails, and a solution may avoid\n958\t$q$. So no sound pruning that the repaired theorems allow is given up. How\n959\toften the published test fires without the repair is measured on small graphs\n960\tonly (20,857 times in 23,978,489 firings, `search_soundness.md` §4.2.4), and\n961\tthe effect on node counts at scale is not yet measured.\n962\t\n963\t### 4.6.3 What has to be redone\n964\t\n965\t- **The solvers.** The definite move and the better move change in the C\n966\t  customer search, in its Python reference, and in the graph pathwidth solver\n967\t  (`../pathwidth_solver/`), as the owner decided.\n968\t- **Re-certification.** Every value in the corpus whose optimality rests only\n969\t  on a customer-search refutation is re-certified with the revised search.\n970\t  Values certified by a checked DRAT refutation through the SAT path, or by a\n971\t  lower bound equal to the value, do not depend on the search and stand as\n972\t  they are. No value is known to be wrong.\n973\t- **The certificate route.** The certificate checker's definite and better\n974\t  steps must check the repaired premises: one subset enumeration or one\n975\t  matching per step. With that change, an accepted certificate proves\n976\t  $Z(M) > k$ by Theorem 4.19's chain without trusting the search, which is the\n977\t  route this paper's dataset relies on for values the SAT path cannot\n978\t  reach.\n979\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b6cad2f-824e-4666-8c2e-24a676325537","uuid":"b2b40ca0-805f-4acd-9edd-48bd0ad5e3cf","timestamp":"2026-10-01T13:11:12.176Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/revised_algorithm.md","content":"# Chu & Stuckey's (revised) algorithm\n\nSection 4 of *The pathwidth complex* (`plan.md` §4) in paper form. It distils\nthe working report `search_soundness.md` (Ralph loop0006, items 05 to 13),\nwhich holds the code references, the full check tables and the history. Every\nstatement here is proved in Lean unless it says otherwise. The Lean names\nrefer to `../lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/`, namespace\n`MOSPFormalization.Search`: seven files (`Basic`, `DefiniteMove`,\n`SubsetRule`, `BetterMove`, `Memo`, `Decide`, `DefiniteMatching`), with no\n`sorry` and no axioms beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`\n(`axiom_check.lean`). Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the\npreprint `../literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf`.\n\n---\n\n## 4.1 Introduction\n\nLet $M$ be a MOSP instance and $k \\ge 0$ an integer. The algorithm of this\nsection decides whether some sequence of the products keeps at most $k$\ncustomer stacks open at once, that is, whether $Z(M) \\le k$. By (E2) of\nsection 3 (Lean: `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`), $Z(M) \\le k$ holds exactly\nwhen the MOSP graph $G_M$ has pathwidth at most $k - 1$. So the algorithm is\nalso a decision procedure for pathwidth. Any graph $G$ is the MOSP graph of the\ninstance with one customer per vertex and one product per edge, each product\nneeded by the two ends of its edge.\n\nThe algorithm is the *customer search* of Chu & Stuckey (2009). It searches\nover the orders in which the customers' stacks close, not over product\nsequences, and it prunes the search with several dominance rules. Chu &\nStuckey state three of these rules as theorems (their Theorems 1, 2 and 3) and\na fourth, the subset rule, in their §2.\n\n**Two of the published theorems are false as stated.** Theorem 1, the\n*definite move*, can discard the last solution at a node. A 14-customer graph\nshows it, and Lean proves the counterexample. Theorem 2, the *better move*,\nrests on Theorem 1 applied one step deeper, and it inherits the fault. Its\nform as the code has run it since 2026-09-26 is still false.\n\n**The revised algorithm** keeps the search and every rule. It replaces the\npremises of Theorems 1 and 2 by a stronger, *hereditary* premise. That premise\nis proved sound, and it is proved equivalent to a bipartite matching\ncondition, so it costs one small matching per candidate. With the two repairs,\nthe whole search is proved sound in Lean: a refutation at $k$ means\n$Z(M) > k$ and $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$.\n\nNo wrong answer of the unrepaired code on a whole instance is known. The gap\nis at the level of a node, and the search has so far always recovered through\nanother branch. Section 4.4.4 states exactly what is proved for the code as it\nstands.\n\n**The owner's decision** (2026-10-01, `plan.md`, *Decisions*). This paper\nstates the gap and the repaired rule. Both solvers, the MOSP customer search\nand the graph pathwidth solver, will be changed to implement the repaired\nrules, and the values that rest only on the customer search will then be\nre-certified.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 The search as a mathematical object\n\n### 4.2.1 Definitions\n\nChu & Stuckey observe that the products matter only through the MOSP graph:\n\"all functions c(p) that produce the same customer graph have the same minimum\nnumber of stacks\" (PDF p. 4). So fix a finite simple graph $G = (C, E)$ whose\nvertices are the customers, and an integer $k \\ge 0$. For a customer $c$ and a\nset $T \\subseteq C$, write\n\n- $N[c] = \\{c\\} \\cup \\{d : cd \\in E\\}$, the closed neighbourhood: the stacks\n  that must be open when $c$'s stack closes;\n- $O(T) = \\bigcup_{c \\in T} N[c]$, the stacks *opened* once the customers of\n  $T$ have closed, with $O(\\emptyset) = \\emptyset$;\n- $\\mathrm{fin}(X) = \\{c : N[c] \\subseteq X\\}$, the customers that could close\n  at no cost once the stacks $X$ are open;\n- $\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{fin}(O(T))$, the *free closure* of $T$;\n- $b(T) = |O(T) \\setminus T|$, the stacks open after $T$ has closed;\n- $o(c, T) = N[c] \\setminus O(T)$, the stacks that closing $c$ next would newly\n  open, and $\\mathrm{open}(c, T) = |o(c, T)|$.\n\nTwo facts are immediate: $T \\subseteq \\mathrm{cl}(T)$ and\n$O(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) = O(T)$ (Lean: `subset_cl`, `opened_cl`). The second says\nthat free closing opens nothing.\n\n**Moves and costs.** A *move* at $T$ closes one customer $c \\notin T$. Its cost\nis the number of stacks open at that moment, counting $c$ itself:\n$$\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) = |O(T \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus T| = b(T \\cup \\{c\\}) + 1.$$\nThis is Chu & Stuckey's $|O(S) - S \\cup o(c, S)|$ (PDF p. 4). The move is\n*playable* when $\\mathrm{cost}(T, c) \\le k$. A *closing order from* $T$ is an\nenumeration $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ of $C \\setminus T$. Its cost is the largest of\nits step costs $\\mathrm{cost}(T \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_{i-1}\\}, c_i)$, and $0$\nwhen $m = 0$. Write $P_k(T)$ when some closing order from $T$ costs at most\n$k$. (Lean: `stepCost`, `orderCost`, `Solvable`;\n`stepCost_eq_openStacks_insert_add_one` for the second form of the cost.)\n\n**States.** On entering a node, the search closes every customer whose\nneighbourhood is already open. One pass suffices, because closing such a\ncustomer opens nothing. So the search only ever stands at a *state*, a set\n$S$ with $S = \\mathrm{cl}(S)$. The root $\\emptyset$ is a state, since every\n$N[c]$ is nonempty. The *child* of a state $S$ by $c \\notin S$ is\n$$S \\cdot c = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{c\\}).$$\n\n**The decision predicate.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ holds when $S = C$, or when some\n$c \\notin S$ has $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$ and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\nThis is the question the search answers: \"is there a run of playable moves,\neach followed by its free moves, that closes everyone?\" (Lean: `SearchSol`,\ndefined inductively and not through $P_k$, so that the link between the two is\na theorem.)\n\n**The invariant.** Every node the search visits satisfies\n$$b(S) = |O(S) \\setminus S| \\le k.$$\nIt holds at the root, where $b(\\emptyset) = 0$. At a child it holds because\n$b(S \\cdot c) \\le |O(S \\cup \\{c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{c\\})| =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$ (Lean: `card_opened_insert_sdiff_lt`). Several\nresults below need it, and it is never an extra assumption about the search.\n\n### 4.2.2 Lemma F: free moves never hurt\n\n**Lemma 4.1 (monotonicity).** If $T \\subseteq T'$ and $O(T') \\subseteq O(T)$,\nthen $P_k(T) \\Rightarrow P_k(T')$.\n\n*Proof.* Take a closing order from $T$ of cost at most $k$ and delete the\ncustomers of $T'$ from it. Each remaining step closes some $c$ at a prefix\n$T' \\cup A$ in place of $T \\cup A$. Its opened set\n$O(T' \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\}) \\subseteq O(T \\cup A \\cup \\{c\\})$ is no larger, and the\nset removed from it, $T' \\cup A \\supseteq T \\cup A$, is no smaller. So no step\ncosts more. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_mono`, via `orderCost_filter_le`.)\n\n**Lemma 4.2 (Lemma F).** If $b(T) \\le k$, then\n$P_k(T) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$. The direction ($\\Rightarrow$)\nneeds no hypothesis. At the root, $P_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$.\n\n*Proof.* ($\\Rightarrow$) By Lemma 4.1 with $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T)$, it suffices\nto show $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ for a state $S$. Induct on\n$|C \\setminus S|$. If $S \\ne C$, let $c$ be the first move of an order from\n$S$ of cost at most $k$. Then $\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) \\le k$, the rest of the\norder gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, Lemma 4.1 gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$, and\ninduction gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$.\n\n($\\Leftarrow$) First, $P_k(\\mathrm{cl}(T)) \\Rightarrow P_k(T)$ when\n$b(T) \\le k$. Close the customers of $\\mathrm{cl}(T) \\setminus T$ one at a\ntime. Each step at a prefix $U$ with $T \\subseteq U$ costs\n$|O(T) \\setminus U| \\le b(T) \\le k$, because the customer closed lies in\n$O(T)$ already. Then follow the order from $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\nSecond, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(S)$ at a state with the invariant,\nby induction on $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. At a move $c$, the child $S \\cdot c$\nsatisfies the invariant, so induction gives $P_k(S \\cdot c)$. The first step\nthen gives $P_k(S \\cup \\{c\\})$, because $b(S \\cup \\{c\\}) =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, c) - 1 < k$. Prepending $c$, at cost at most $k$, gives\n$P_k(S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_searchSol_cl`,\n`searchSol_cl_of_solvable`, `solvable_of_searchSol`, `solvable_cl_iff`;\nat the root, `solvable_empty_iff`.)\n\nThe hypothesis is needed. On $K_2$ with one customer closed and $k = 0$,\n$\\mathrm{cl}(T)$ is everything, so $\\mathrm{Sol}_0(\\mathrm{cl}(T))$ holds, but\nclosing the other customer costs one stack.\n\n*In plain English.* Once every neighbour of a customer has an open stack, that\ncustomer can close straight away without opening anything new. Doing so never\nmakes later steps dearer, and it makes some cheaper, because one fewer stack\nis left open. So a search that always closes such customers first loses\nnothing. The only proviso is that the stacks already open must fit within the\nbudget, since closing the free customers one by one costs exactly that many.\n\n### 4.2.3 Cost is narrowness, so the search decides pathwidth\n\nFor a full closing order $\\tau = c_1, \\dots, c_n$ from $\\emptyset$, write\n$S_i = \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$ and\n$\\partial X = \\{v \\notin X : v \\text{ has a neighbour in } X\\}$. The $i$-th step\ncosts\n$$|O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}| = 1 + |\\partial S_i|,$$\nbecause $O(S_i) \\setminus S_{i-1}$ is $c_i$ together with the customers\noutside $S_i$ that have a neighbour in $S_i$. The maximum of these numbers over\n$i$ is the *narrowness of the sequence* $\\tau$, in the dual shack process of\nKornai & Tuza (1992), formalised as `Complex.outNarrowness`.\n\n**Theorem 4.3 (the search decides pathwidth and MOSP).** For nonempty $C$,\n$$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset) \\iff \\min_\\tau \\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) \\le k \\iff \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 \\le k.$$\nFor a MOSP instance $M$ with at least one requirement, $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$\non $G_M$ holds iff $Z(M) \\le k$.\n\n*Proof.* The first equivalence is Lemma 4.2 at the root. For the second, read\n$\\tau$ backwards as a layout $L$, so that the cut after position $n - i$ of\n$L$ separates $C \\setminus S_i$ (left) from $S_i$ (right). The left vertices\nwith a right neighbour are exactly $\\partial S_i$. So\n$\\max_{1 \\le i < n} |\\partial S_i|$ is the vertex separation of $L$, and the\nlast step ($i = n$, $\\partial C = \\emptyset$) costs 1. Hence\n$\\mathrm{cost}(\\tau) = \\mathrm{vs}(L) + 1$. Minimising over $\\tau$ gives\n$\\mathrm{vs}(G) + 1 = \\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1$ by (E1). The MOSP form is (E2).\n$\\square$ (The Lean statement counts the active suffix of $\\tau$ itself, the\ndevelopment's convention, so no reversal appears there.) (Lean: `orderCost_ofFn_eq_outNarrowness`,\n`orderCost_ofFn_eq_vertexSepOfLayout_add_one`,\n`searchSol_empty_iff_narrowness_le`,\n`searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le`,\n`searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le`; behind them\n`Complex.narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one` and\n`mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.)\n\nSo the only thing a refutation, $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$, may mean is\n$Z(M) > k$, equivalently $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$. The rest of this section\nshows that the pruning rules do not change the answer.\n\n*In plain English.* Close the customers one at a time. When a customer\ncloses, every neighbour must already have its stack open, so the stacks open\nat that moment are the customer itself plus every not-yet-closed customer\ntouching the closed group. Read the closing order backwards and this is the\ncount that vertex separation takes at each cut. So the best closing order uses\nexactly vertex separation plus one stacks, which is pathwidth plus one.\n\n**A modelling remark.** The code drops customers with no product. In $G_M$\nthey are isolated vertices, which are free at the root and change nothing.\nThis is argued, not formalised.\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 The rules\n\n### 4.3.0 What a rule must satisfy\n\nAt a node the search has a state $S$ with the invariant, and a set $Q$ of\n*old moves* (section 4.3.5). Its *candidates* are the customers outside\n$S \\cup Q$. The *cost cut* keeps the playable candidates $P$, in increasing\ncustomer index. The cost cut is exact, since an unplayable move is not a move\nof $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$. A *filter* then keeps a sublist $L \\subseteq P$, and the\nsearch recurses on $S \\cdot c$ for $c \\in L$.\n\n**Node soundness.** Suppose $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$ for every\n$q \\in Q$. The filter is node-sound at $(S, Q)$ if $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$ implies\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$ for some $c \\in L$ (Lean: `NodeSoundAt`;\n`FilterSound` when it holds at every node with the invariant).\n\nEach rule is justified by a *covering*: a discarded move $r$ is covered by $c$\nwhen $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot c)$. A node\nis sound when every link a rule cites is a true covering and every chain of\ncited links from a discarded candidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$ (Lean:\n`nodeSoundAt_of_covering`, with `Covered` for the chains). The second\ncondition can fail even when every link is true, through a cycle. Section\n4.3.4 has the example.\n\nThe rules run in the code's order: the free move on entry; the memo; the\nold-move candidate set; the cost cut; then the *definite move*, the *subset\nrule* and the *better move*, each once.\n\n### 4.3.1 The free move\n\n**Published form.** Chu & Stuckey have no separate rule. It is the\n$\\mathrm{open} = 0$ case of their Theorem 1.\n\n**Precise form.** On entering a node at $T$, move to $\\mathrm{cl}(T)$.\n\n**Soundness.** Lemma 4.2. The search's predicate is defined over states, and\nby Lemma 4.2 it agrees with the plain question $P_k$ at every node the search\nvisits.\n\n### 4.3.2 The definite move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 1.** Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and\nclose(q, S) ≥ open(q, S), then if U′ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a\nsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.1, PDF p. 6). Here close(q, S) =\n|{d | o(d, S) ⊆ o(q, S)}| (PDF p. 4).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** At a state $S$, let $q \\in P$ and let\n$$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |\\{d \\in C \\setminus S : o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)\\}|,$$\nwhich counts $q$ itself and counts customers in $Q$. (Read literally, the\npaper's $d$ ranges over all customers. A closed $d$ has $o(d, S) = \\emptyset$\nand would always be counted, so the code's reading, $d \\notin S$, is the\nsensible one.) If $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$, the filter\nkeeps $L = [q]$ for the first such $q$ in index order, and nothing else runs at\nthe node (Lean: `closeCount`, `IsDefinite`).\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\nWrite $X = S \\cdot q = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ throughout.\n\n**Lemma 4.4 (what the premise says).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n$\\mathrm{close}(q, S) = |X \\setminus S|$. Hence the premise\n$\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$ holds iff $b(X) \\le b(S)$: the\nchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\n\n*Proof.* For $d \\notin S$: $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$ iff\n$N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup N[q] = O(S \\cup \\{q\\})$ iff $d \\in X$. For the second\nclaim, $O(X) = O(S) \\cup N[q]$ gives\n$b(X) = |O(S)| + \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - |X|$, while $b(S) = |O(S)| - |S|$, and\n$|X| - |S| = \\mathrm{close}(q, S)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `closeCount_eq`,\n`isDefinite_iff`.)\n\n**Counterexample 4.5.** Let $G$ be the graph on customers $0, \\dots, 13$ with\nthe 26 edges\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n&0\\text{–}3,\\ 0\\text{–}4,\\ 0\\text{–}7,\\ 0\\text{–}11,\\ 1\\text{–}2,\\ 1\\text{–}5,\\ 1\\text{–}6,\\ 2\\text{–}3,\\ 2\\text{–}4,\\ 5\\text{–}9,\\ 5\\text{–}11,\\ 5\\text{–}12,\\ 6\\text{–}8,\\\\\n&6\\text{–}13,\\ 7\\text{–}9,\\ 7\\text{–}10,\\ 7\\text{–}13,\\ 8\\text{–}9,\\ 8\\text{–}10,\\ 8\\text{–}11,\\ 9\\text{–}10,\\ 9\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}11,\\ 10\\text{–}12,\\ 10\\text{–}13,\\ 12\\text{–}13,\n\\end{aligned}$$\nand take $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 6$, $q = 0$. Then:\n\n- $S$ is a state, $O(S) = N[2] = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $b(S) = 3 \\le 6$;\n- $o(0, S) = \\{0, 7, 11\\}$, so $\\mathrm{open}(0, S) = 3$, and\n  $\\mathrm{cost}(S, 0) = |\\{0, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11\\}| = 6$, so $0$ is playable;\n- the customers $d \\notin S$ with $o(d, S) \\subseteq \\{0, 7, 11\\}$ are $0$,\n  $3$ and $4$ (both of the latter have $o = \\{0\\}$), so\n  $\\mathrm{close}(0, S) = 3 \\ge \\mathrm{open}(0, S)$;\n- $0$ is the first customer in index order, so the code keeps $\\{0\\}$ alone;\n- $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(S)$ holds: the order $1, 3, 4, 6, 12, 13, 0, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11$\n  costs at most 6;\n- the child is $X = \\{0, 2, 3, 4\\}$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_6(X)$ fails: only seven\n  sets are reachable from $X$ by moves of cost at most 6, and none of them is\n  $C$.\n\n![The counterexample to Theorem 1](figures/definite_counterexample.png)\n\n*Figure 4.1. The graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the state where the definite\nmove fails. Customer 2 is closed, and 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens the\nnew stacks 0, 7 and 11, and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the rule\nkeeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them: closing\nthem first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution starting\nwith 0 cannot match. Regenerate with `python -m paper2.counterexample_figure`,\nwhich recomputes every labelled fact from the instance (PDF beside it).*\n\nSo Theorem 1 is false, both as published and as coded. (Lean:\n`definiteMove_counterexample`, `cexGraph`, `cex_solvable`, and\n`cexFamily_closed` for the seven sets, checked by `decide +kernel`.) The published implication itself is refuted as a universal statement, with\nthe bridge from the free-closed child to $S \\cup \\{q\\}$ inside the proof,\nunder the code's reading of $\\mathrm{close}$ and under the literal one, which\nalso counts closed customers and so is a weaker premise\n(`chuStuckey_theorem1_false`, `chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal`,\n`Search/PublishedTheorems.lean`).\n\n**Why the published proof fails.** The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\nsolution $U' = S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [c_1, \\dots, c_m, q, \\dots]$ and claims that\nafter each prefix \"we have at most open(q, S) extra stacks open, but at least\nclose(q, S) extra stacks closed\" (PDF p. 6). Compare the two runs at a prefix\n$T \\supseteq S$ of $U'$ that does not yet contain $q$. Moving $q$ forward\nchanges the open stacks from $b(T)$ to $b(T \\cup X)$, and\n$$b(T \\cup X) - b(T) = |N[q] \\setminus O(T)| - |X \\setminus T|.$$\nAt $T = S$ this is $\\mathrm{open} - \\mathrm{close} \\le 0$. But the customers of\n$X$ that $U'$ has *already* closed are in $T$, so they are no longer extra\nstacks closed, while the stacks $q$ opens may still be new. In the example,\n$U'$ may begin with $3$, after which $4$ is free and $T = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$. Then\n$N[0] \\setminus O(T) = \\{7, 11\\}$ and $X \\setminus T = \\{0\\}$, so moving $0$\nforward costs one more stack: $b(T) = 2$ and $b(T \\cup X) = 3$. Customers $3$\nand $4$ each counted once in $\\mathrm{close}(0, S)$, but they share the single\nnew stack $0$, and a solution that closes them first gains two closed stacks\nfor one opened.\n\nThere is a second, independent slip. Even where the conclusion holds, the\nliteral sequence $S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q, c_1, \\dots]$ need not be a solution,\nbecause a customer that $q$ makes free stays open until its turn comes. With\nedges $0\\text{–}3$ and $1\\text{–}2$, $S = \\emptyset$ and $q = 0$\n($\\mathrm{open} = \\mathrm{close} = 2$), the order $1, 2, 0, 3$ costs 2 but\n$0, 1, 2, 3$ costs 3. Closing $3$ right after $0$ fixes it. The argument needs\nthe free moves.\n\n**Repair.** Call $q \\notin S$ *hereditarily definite* at $S$ when\n$$b(X) \\le b(B) \\quad \\text{for every } B \\text{ with } S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X \\text{ and } q \\notin B.$$\nBy Lemma 4.4, the code's premise is the case $B = S$. The repair asks that no\nintermediate set avoiding $q$ is a better place to stand than $X$ (Lean:\n`IsHereditarilyDefinite`; `IsHereditarilyDefinite.isDefinite`). In the\ncounterexample it fails at $B = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, where $b(B) = 2 < 3 = b(X)$\n(Lean: `not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex`).\n\n**Lemma 4.6 (open stacks are submodular).** For all $A, B \\subseteq C$,\n$$b(A \\cup B) + b(A \\cap B) \\le b(A) + b(B).$$\n\n*Proof.* Since $T \\subseteq O(T)$, $b(T) = |O(T)| - |T|$. Now\n$O(A \\cup B) = O(A) \\cup O(B)$ and $O(A \\cap B) \\subseteq O(A) \\cap O(B)$, so\n$|O(A \\cup B)| + |O(A \\cap B)| \\le |O(A)| + |O(B)|$. Subtract\n$|A \\cup B| + |A \\cap B| = |A| + |B|$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`openStacks_submodular`, from `openStacks_add_card`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Count the stacks opened by two groups of closed customers.\nThe union of the groups opens exactly what either opens, and their overlap\nopens at most what both open. So the two counts on the left can only fall\nshort of the two on the right, and subtracting the customers themselves, who\nbalance exactly, keeps that.\n\n**Theorem 4.7 (the repaired definite move is sound).** If $q \\notin S$ is\nhereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S) \\Rightarrow P_k(X)$, with no other\nhypothesis. At a state with the invariant,\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, so keeping only a\nplayable hereditarily definite $q$ is sound.\n\n*Proof.* Take a closing order $c_1, \\dots, c_m$ from $S$ of cost at most $k$,\nwith prefixes $T_i = S \\cup \\{c_1, \\dots, c_i\\}$, and let $c_j = q$. Build an\norder from $X$ by following $c_1, \\dots, c_{j-1}$ and skipping any customer\nalready in $X$. For $i < j$ with $c_i \\notin X$, the step closes $c_i$ at\n$T_{i-1} \\cup X$ and costs $b(T_i \\cup X) + 1$. Put $Z = T_i$. Then\n$S \\subseteq Z \\cap X \\subseteq X$ and $q \\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n$b(X) \\le b(Z \\cap X)$, and Lemma 4.6 gives\n$$b(Z \\cup X) \\le b(Z) + b(X) - b(Z \\cap X) \\le b(Z).$$\nSo the step costs at most $b(T_i) + 1 = \\mathrm{cost}(T_{i-1}, c_i) \\le k$.\nAfter these steps the closed set is $T_{j-1} \\cup X$. It contains $T_j$, and\nsince $S \\subseteq T_{j-1}$ its opened set is $O(T_{j-1}) \\cup N[q] = O(T_j)$.\nThe rest of the original order gives $P_k(T_j)$, so Lemma 4.1 gives\n$P_k(T_{j-1} \\cup X)$, and together $P_k(X)$.\n\nFor the search's form, the invariant and Lemma 4.2 give $P_k(S)$, hence\n$P_k(X)$, hence $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(X)) = \\mathrm{Sol}_k(X)$ by Lemma 4.2\nagain. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`, by\n`solvable_union_cl_of_hereditarilyDefinite` and `stepCost_union_cl_le`;\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Take any good closing order and imagine closing $q$, with\neverything it makes free, before anything else. At each moment before the\norder reaches $q$, compare the two situations. The hereditary condition says\nthat standing on the \"$q$ and its free customers\" side never leaves more stacks\nopen than standing at any point between where you started and there, provided\n$q$ itself has not closed. Submodularity turns that into a step-by-step\nguarantee: the order with $q$ first never has more stacks open than the\noriginal. Once the original order closes $q$, the two have the same stacks\nopen and the shifted one has closed at least as much.\n\n**Corollary.** Every $q$ with $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) \\le 1$ is hereditarily\ndefinite, so the code's rule is right in those cases. For\n$S \\subseteq B \\subseteq X$ with $q \\notin B$, the stacks in $O(X)$ but not in\n$O(B)$ lie in $N[q] \\setminus O(S) = o(q, S)$, so there is at most one of\nthem. Since $O(B) \\subseteq O(X)$, $b(X) - b(B) = |O(X) \\setminus O(B)| -\n|X \\setminus B| \\le 1 - 1 = 0$, because $q \\in X \\setminus B$. (Lean: `isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one`.)\n\n**The matching form.** The repair quantifies over all sets $B$. An equivalent\nform is cheap. Let $Y = X \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$, the customers the child\ncloses besides $q$, and for $D \\subseteq Y$ let\n$o(D) = \\bigcup_{d \\in D} o(d, S)$. Every $d \\in Y$ has\n$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(q, S)$, so consider the bipartite graph joining each\n$d \\in Y$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n\n**Theorem 4.8 (the repair is a matching condition).** For every $S$ and $q$,\n$q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff some set $M \\subseteq Y$ with\n$|M| \\ge \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ can be matched injectively to stacks\n$f(d) \\in o(d, S)$. Equivalently, the largest matching of the bipartite graph\nhas at least $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$ edges.\n\n*Proof.* Let $q \\notin S$; if $q \\in S$ then $\\mathrm{open}(q, S) = 0$ and both\nsides hold trivially. The sets $B$ of the repair are exactly $B = S \\cup D$\nwith $D \\subseteq Y$. Since $|O(S \\cup D)| = |O(S)| + |o(D)|$ and\n$|X| = |S| + 1 + |Y|$,\n$$b(X) \\le b(S \\cup D) \\iff \\mathrm{open}(q, S) + |D| \\le |o(D)| + |Y| + 1 \\iff |D| - |o(D)| \\le \\delta,$$\nwith $\\delta = |Y| + 1 - \\mathrm{open}(q, S)$. (At $D = \\emptyset$ this gives\n$\\delta \\ge 0$.) By the deficiency form of Hall's theorem (Ore 1955), the\nlargest matching of $Y$ into the stacks has size\n$|Y| - \\max_{D \\subseteq Y} (|D| - |o(D)|)$. So the repair holds iff that\nmaximum is at most $\\delta$, iff the largest matching has size at least\n$|Y| - \\delta = \\mathrm{open}(q, S) - 1$. $\\square$\n\nThe Lean proof uses the textbook reduction of the deficiency form to Hall's\ntheorem: give every $d \\in Y$ the same $\\delta$ dummy stacks besides $o(d, S)$,\ncheck Hall's condition from the deficiency inequality, apply Mathlib's\n`Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_existsInjective'`, and keep the customers\nmatched to real stacks, of which at most $\\delta$ are lost. (Lean:\n`HasDefiniteMatching`, `card_opened_union_eq`, `card_cl_insert`,\n`hall_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`, `exists_matching_of_isHereditarilyDefinite`,\n`isHereditarilyDefinite_of_matching`, and\n`isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching`.)\n\n*In plain English.* Closing $q$ opens some new stacks, and it lets some other\ncustomers close for free, each of whom needed one or more of those new stacks.\nThe rule is safe exactly when you can pair all but one of the new stacks with\na different free customer who needed it. In the counterexample, closing $0$\nopens three new stacks, $0$, $7$ and $11$, and frees customers $3$ and $4$.\nBoth of them needed only stack $0$, so only one pairing is possible where two\nare required.\n\n### 4.3.3 The subset rule\n\n**Published form.** \"if o(cᵢ, S) ⊆ o(cⱼ, S) and i < j, then clearly, we can\nalways play i before j rather than playing j immediately, since closing j will\nclose i in any case. Hence move j can be removed from the candidates R\nconsidered for the next customer to close\" (§2, PDF p. 5).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** Say $d$ *dominates* $r$ at $S$ when $d \\ne r$,\n$o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, and either the inclusion is strict or $d < r$.\nThe rule drops every $r \\in P$ dominated by some $d \\in C \\setminus S$, where\n$d$ ranges over all remaining customers, including those in $Q$. If every\ncandidate would be dropped, it keeps $P$ whole (the *fallback*). It runs only\nwhen the definite move did not fire (Lean: `Dominates`, `IsSubsetDominated`,\n`subsetFilter`).\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$\nfor the dominator $d$. **Sound as coded.**\n\n**Lemma 4.9 (the covering).** If $o(d, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$ and\n$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot d)$, for every set\n$S$, with no invariant and no tie-break. Moreover $d$ is playable.\n\n*Proof.* $N[d] \\subseteq O(S) \\cup o(d, S) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$, so\n$d \\in S \\cdot r$, and $O(S \\cup \\{d\\}) \\subseteq O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ gives\n$S \\cdot d \\subseteq S \\cdot r$ and $\\mathrm{cost}(S, d) \\le \\mathrm{cost}(S, r)$.\nIf $r \\in S \\cdot d$, the two opened sets are equal and $S \\cdot d = S \\cdot r$.\nOtherwise close $r$ at $S \\cdot d$. It costs\n$|O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot d| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{r\\}) \\setminus S| =\n\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$, and it lands on\n$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S \\cup \\{r\\})) = S \\cdot r$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`, with\n`mem_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`cl_insert_subset_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`cl_insert_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset`,\n`stepCost_le_of_newlyOpened_subset`.)\n\n**Proposition 4.10 (node soundness).** At any state $S \\ne C$, if every\n$q \\in Q$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$, the subset filter is node-sound.\nWhen a solution exists, the fallback never fires.\n\n*Proof.* Domination is irreflexive and transitive, so it is a strict order.\nGiven $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, some playable $r$ has $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r)$,\nand $r \\notin Q$. Among the customers $m \\notin S$ with\n$o(m, S) \\subseteq o(r, S)$, take the least by $(|o(m, S)|, m)$. Any dominator\nof $m$ would come earlier in that order, so $m$ is undominated. By Lemma 4.9,\n$m$ is playable and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot m)$ holds, so $m \\notin Q$. So $m$\nis a candidate that survives. $\\square$ (Lean: `not_dominates_self`,\n`Dominates.trans`, `exists_undominated`, `subsetKept_nonempty`,\n`subsetFilter_sound`.)\n\n*In plain English.* If closing $d$ opens only stacks that closing $r$ would\nopen anyway, then closing $r$ makes $d$ free. So closing $d$ first, then $r$,\nreaches the same place as closing $r$, never at a higher cost, and the search\nmay as well try $d$. Follow such domination downwards and you reach a customer\nnobody dominates, which the rule keeps.\n\n**The tie-break is necessary (Counterexample 4.11).** Without it, two\ncustomers with equal new stacks dominate each other and both go. Take the\n7-customer graph with edges $0\\text{–}2$, $0\\text{–}3$, $0\\text{–}6$,\n$1\\text{–}4$, $1\\text{–}6$, $3\\text{–}6$, $4\\text{–}5$, $4\\text{–}6$, the\nstate $S = \\{2\\}$ and $k = 3$. The playable candidates are $\\{0, 3, 5\\}$, with\n$o(0, S) = o(3, S) = \\{3, 6\\}$ and $o(5, S) = \\{4, 5\\}$. Without the tie-break\n$0$ and $3$ both go, $5$ survives, so the fallback does not fire, and the\nfilter keeps $\\{5\\}$. A solution exists from $S$ ($0, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6$), but none\nfrom $S \\cdot 5 = \\{2, 5\\}$, where no move costs at most 3. With the tie-break,\nonly $3$ goes and the filter keeps $\\{0, 5\\}$. (Lean:\n`noTieBreak_counterexample`, `tieGraph`, `WeakDominates`, `weakSubsetFilter`.)\nThe paper's own form, which requires $d < r$ even for a strict inclusion, is a\nsubrelation of the code's, uses the same covering and is acyclic by index. It\nis checked, not stated in Lean.\n\n### 4.3.4 The better move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 2.** Suppose S ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are\nplayable and close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r}) then if U′ = S ++ [r] ++ R is\na solution there exists a solution U = S ++ [q] ++ R′\" (§3.2, PDF p. 6). The\nproof begins: \"The conditions imply that if r is played now, q becomes a\ndefinite move.\"\n\n**Precise form, as coded** (the C only, since 2026-09-26). The input is the\nlist $W$ of subset survivors, in index order. Let $X_r = O(S \\cup \\{r\\})$. A\nmember $r$ is dropped if some earlier $q \\in W$, among the first $L$ of $W$ if\n$L > 0$, satisfies\n\n- *(premise 3)* $|(X_r \\cup N[q]) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k$, and\n- *(premise 4)* $\\mathrm{open}' \\le \\mathrm{close}'$, where\n  $\\mathrm{open}' = |N[q] \\setminus X_r|$ and $\\mathrm{close}'$ counts the\n  $d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ with $\\emptyset \\ne N[d] \\setminus X_r \\subseteq N[q] \\setminus X_r$.\n\nPremise 2 is $q \\in W \\subseteq P$. Whether $q$ itself survives is not\nconsulted. (Lean: `IsBetter`, `betterCite`, `withinLimit`, `betterFilterBy`.)\n\n**Claimed soundness.** $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\n**Lemma 4.12 (the premise is Theorem 1 at the child).** Premises 3 and 4 hold\niff $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q) \\le k$ and the code's definite premise\nholds for $q$ at the child $T = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$.\n\n*Proof.* Premise 3 is $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{r\\}, q)$ by definition. Since\n$O(T) = X_r$, $\\mathrm{open}' = \\mathrm{open}(q, T)$. A customer\n$d \\notin S \\cup \\{r\\}$ has $N[d] \\setminus X_r \\ne \\emptyset$ exactly when\n$d \\notin T$, and then $N[d] \\setminus X_r = o(d, T)$. So\n$\\mathrm{close}' = \\mathrm{close}(q, T)$. $\\square$ (Lean: `isBetter_iff`,\n`betterOpen_eq`, `betterClose_eq`.)\n\nSo the better move is Theorem 1 at the child, followed by a swap of $q$ and\n$r$. The swap is sound. The appeal to Theorem 1 is not.\n\n**Counterexample 4.13.** Take the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at the root,\n$S = \\emptyset$, with $k = 6$ and $Q = \\emptyset$. No playable customer meets\nthe definite premise, so the definite move does not fire. Customers $0$ and $2$\nboth survive the subset rule, $0 < 2$, and premises 3 and 4 hold for $r = 2$,\n$q = 0$, so the code drops $2$ citing $0$. But $S \\cdot 2 = \\{2\\}$ has a\nsolution (Counterexample 4.5), and $S \\cdot 0 = \\{0\\}$ has none. The child\n$\\{2\\}$ is exactly the state at which Theorem 1 wrongly moves $0$ forward.\n(Lean: `betterMove_counterexample`, by an invariant family of 18 sets,\n`bmFamily_closed`.) Theorem 2's published implication is refuted the same way, under both\nreadings of $\\mathrm{close}$ (`chuStuckey_theorem2_false`,\n`chuStuckey_theorem2_false_literal`). At this node the filter still keeps customer $1$, which has\na solution, so this is a *false link*, not a lost node (Lean:\n`betterMove_counterexample_node`). No node where the better move loses the last\nsolution has been found (section 4.5).\n\n**Repair.** Cite $q$ for $r$ only if premise 3 holds and $q$ is hereditarily\ndefinite at $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$ (Lean: `IsRepairedBetter`;\n`IsRepairedBetter.isBetter` shows it implies the code's premise). By Theorem\n4.8 this is one matching at the child.\n\n**Theorem 4.14 (the repaired better move is sound).** If $r, q \\notin S$,\n$\\mathrm{cost}(S, r) \\le k$ and the repaired premise holds, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot r) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$.\n\n*Proof.* Let $T = S \\cdot r$, which has the invariant because $r$ is playable.\nIf $q \\in T$, put $T' = T$. Otherwise Theorem 4.7 at $T$ gives\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(T')$ for $T' = \\mathrm{cl}(T \\cup \\{q\\})$. Now let\n$Z = S \\cdot q$. Both $\\mathrm{cl}(Z \\cup \\{r\\})$ and $T'$ equal\n$\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[r])$. If $r \\in Z$ then $Z = T'$. Otherwise\nclosing $r$ at $Z$ costs\n$$|O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus Z| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| = |O(S \\cup \\{q, r\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{r\\})| \\le k,$$\nwhere the middle equality exchanges $q$ and $r$, both of which are opened and\nneither of which is in $S$, and the last step is premise 3. So $r$ is a\nplayable move from $Z$ to $T'$, and $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(Z)$ follows. $\\square$\n(Lean: `solvable_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`,\n`searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter`, with `card_sdiff_insert_eq`.) The\nimplication needs $r$ playable, not $q$. Playability of $q$ is what makes $q$ a\nchild of the search.\n\n*In plain English.* The better move says: \"if closing $r$ now would make $q$ a\nsafe next move, then closing $q$ first and $r$ second is at least as good as\nclosing $r$ first.\" The swap at the end is fine, because both orders end in the\nsame place and neither step overflows. What fails is \"would make $q$ a safe\nnext move\", which was Theorem 1. With the repaired test there, the argument\ngoes through unchanged.\n\n**Two earlier faults, now proved.** The 2026-09-26 fix corrected two bugs in\nthis rule, both caught by testing (`../reports/better_move_bug.md` §7). Lean\nnow proves a counterexample to each.\n\n- *Bug A, the close count.* Counting also the customers that $r$ finishes on\n  its own makes the rule's own conclusion false, on a 12-customer graph at\n  $k = 4$ (Lean: `IsBetterOld`, `bugA_counterexample`, `bugAGraph`).\n- *Bug B, the order of the rules.* Running the better move before the subset\n  rule lets the subset rule cite a customer the better move has already\n  dropped. On an 8-customer graph at $S = \\{2\\}$, $k = 4$, the better move drops\n  $3$ citing $0$ and the subset rule drops $0$ citing $3$. Every premise is the\n  corrected one and true, and the node is lost through the cycle (Lean:\n  `oldOrderFilter`, `bugB_counterexample`, `bugBGraph`).\n\n### 4.3.5 The old move\n\n**Published form.** \"**Theorem 3.** Let S = [s₁, s₂, …, sₙ]. Suppose that\nS′ = [s₁, s₂, .., sₘ, q, sₘ₊₁, …, sₙ] is playable, then if U′ = S ++ [q] ++ R is\na solution then U = S′ ++ R is a solution\" (§3.3, PDF p. 7), with the rule\n\"if it is found that at some ancestor node, the q branch has been searched and\nU is playable, then q can immediately be pruned\".\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** A node carries a set $Q$ of old moves, empty at the\nroot. When a candidate's subtree returns *false* without aborting, the\ncandidate joins the node's list `seen`. A later sibling $c'$ passes to its child\nthose $q \\in$ `seen` with\n$$\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c') = |(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c']) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k,$$\nwhich is the test that the step $c'$ stays playable with $q$ reinserted before\nit. Old moves are removed from the candidates (Lean: `Inherits`, `pathEnd`).\n\n**Claimed soundness.** Every $q \\in Q$ at a node has\n$\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. **Sound as coded.**\n\n**Lemma 4.15 (reinsertion).** If $\\mathrm{cost}(S \\cup \\{q\\}, c) \\le k$, then\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)) \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\nfor every set $S$, with no invariant. Iterated along a path whose every move\npasses the test: if the $q$ branch at an ancestor has no solution, neither has\n$q$ at the end of the path.\n\n*Proof.* Both $\\mathrm{cl}(\\{q\\} \\cup S \\cdot c)$ and\n$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{c\\} \\cup S \\cdot q)$ equal $\\mathrm{fin}(O(S) \\cup N[q] \\cup N[c])$.\nIf $c \\in S \\cdot q$, then the second is $S \\cdot q$ itself. Otherwise $c$ is a\nmove at $S \\cdot q$ of cost\n$|O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus S \\cdot q| \\le |O(S \\cup \\{q, c\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})| \\le k$,\nbecause $S \\cup \\{q\\} \\subseteq S \\cdot q$. Induction on the path gives the\nsecond statement. $\\square$ (Lean: `searchSol_reinsert`,\n`cl_insert_cl_insert_comm`, `stepCost_cl_le`, `searchSol_reinsert_path`,\n`not_searchSol_of_oldMove`.)\n\n**The test is necessary.** On the path $4\\text{–}0\\text{–}2\\text{–}1\\text{–}3$\nwith $S = \\emptyset$, $q = 2$, $c = 3$ and $k = 2$, the move $c$ is playable but\n$\\mathrm{cost}(\\{2\\}, 3) = 3$. Here\n$\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\mathrm{cl}(\\{2\\} \\cup \\mathrm{cl}\\{3\\})) = \\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{1, 2, 3\\})$\nholds and $\\mathrm{Sol}_2(\\{2\\})$ does not. This is the smallest such graph,\nby exhaustion to 5 vertices (Lean: `reinsert_needs_test`). Chu & Stuckey make\nthe same point: the condition \"is in fact crucial\" (PDF p. 7).\n\n*In plain English.* Suppose that at some earlier node the search tried closing\n$q$ and found no way to finish. Since then it has closed some other customers.\nIf, at every one of those steps, closing $q$ first would still have kept the\nstep within budget, then \"close $q$ now\" leads to a place the search could also\nhave reached from the earlier attempt. That attempt failed, so this one fails\ntoo, and $q$ can be skipped.\n\nThe paper's \"synergy\" (adding $r$ to $Q$ when an old move is better than $r$)\nis implemented in neither the C nor the Python and is not covered.\n\n### 4.3.6 The memo\n\n**Published form.** Nogood recording: \"if it failed, we record the nogood and\nreturn false when we revisit it\" (PDF p. 4).\n\n**Precise form, as coded.** When a node's loop finishes with no child answering\n*true* and without an abort, its closed set $S$ is recorded. At any later node\nwith the same closed set the search answers *false* at once. The memo may\nstore fewer entries (a limit, a load factor), which only stores less.\n\n**Claimed soundness.** A recorded $S$ has $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n\n**Lemma 4.16 (the key).** If $b(T) \\le k$, $b(T') \\le k$ and\n$\\mathrm{cl}(T) = \\mathrm{cl}(T')$, then $P_k(T) \\iff P_k(T')$. So a state\nrefuted once is refuted whatever path reached it.\n\n*Proof.* Lemma 4.2 at both sides. $\\square$ (Lean: `solvable_iff_of_cl_eq`.)\n\nThe memo is sound once every recorded entry is a genuine refutation. With old\nmove on, that is not a property of the node alone, and it is proved for the\nwhole run in section 4.4.2.\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 The composition and the theorem\n\n### 4.4.1 The filter\n\nThe filter runs in the order\n\n$$\\text{definite move} \\;\\to\\; \\text{subset rule} \\;\\to\\; \\text{better move},$$\n\neach once, and each cites only customers still standing or in $Q$. If the\ndefinite move fires, $L = [q]$. Otherwise the subset rule runs over $P$ with\ndominators from all of $C \\setminus S$, while nothing has yet been discarded.\nThe better move then runs over the subset survivors and cites only an earlier\nsurvivor (Lean: `fullFilter`; `codeFullFilter` with the code's premises,\n`repairedFullFilter` with both repairs).\n\n**Proposition 4.17 (the repaired filter is node-sound).** At every state with\nthe invariant, for every limit $L$ and every set $Q$ of refuted old moves,\n$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$ with both repairs is\nnode-sound.\n\n*Proof.* If the definite move fires on a playable hereditarily definite $q$,\nTheorem 4.7 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Otherwise Proposition 4.10 gives\na subset survivor with a solution. Let $w$ be the earliest such survivor in\nindex order. If the better move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor $q$\nunder the repaired premise, and Theorem 4.14 gives $\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$,\nwhich contradicts the choice of $w$. So $w \\in L$. $\\square$ (Lean:\n`betterFilterBy_sound`, `fullFilter_sound`, `repairedFilter_sound`,\n`repairedFullFilter_sound`.)\n\nThe argument needs no chain of citations and does not use $L$. Bug B shows\nthat the order matters. With the better move first, a subset link can point\nbackwards at a customer already discarded, and every premise can be true while\nthe chain cycles.\n\n*In plain English.* If the safe-move test fires, the one move it keeps is\nproved safe. If it does not, the subset rule always keeps some move that still\nleads to a solution. Among the moves that do, look at the first one. The better\nmove can only throw it out by pointing at an even earlier move that it proves\nat least as good, and there is none. So something that still works always\nsurvives.\n\n### 4.4.2 Runs, the memo and old move together\n\nA run of the search is described by a big-step semantics of its *false*\nanswers (Lean: `Exec`). A node intersects $Q$ with its remaining customers,\nenumerates the filter's output in any order, runs each child with any subset of\nthe old moves that pass the reinsertion test, adds each refuted child to\n`seen`, and records its state in the memo or not. A memo hit answers *false*.\nAn aborted run produces no `Exec` at all, which matches the code: an abort\nanswers *unknown* and records nothing. So the child order, the memo policy and\nold move on or off are all covered.\n\n**Theorem 4.18 (runs are sound).** Let the filter be node-sound and return only\nplayable customers. Suppose that at the start of a run the invariant holds at\n$S$, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and every $q \\in Q \\setminus S$\nhas $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S \\cdot q)$. Then the run leaves only genuine memo\nentries, and if it answers *false* then $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$.\n\n*Proof.* Induct on the run, which follows the order in which subtrees complete.\nEach entry of `seen` at a node is either a sibling subtree that has already\ncompleted with *false*, refuted by the induction hypothesis, or one of the\nnode's own old moves, refuted by hypothesis. Each old move a child inherits\npassed the test, so Lemma 4.15 makes it genuinely refuted at the child. When a\nnode's loop has refuted every member of the filter's output, node soundness\ngives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(S)$, so recording $S$ is correct. A memo hit is\ncorrect by the hypothesis on the memo. $\\square$ (Lean: `Exec.sound`,\n`exec_root_sound`; `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` with\n`repairedFullFilter_filterSound` and `repairedFullFilter_playable`.)\n\n**Why one implementation refused the combination.** The Python reference drops\nthe memo under old move, on the ground that a failure reached with old-move\npruning depends on the branches an ancestor searched. What is true in this is\nlocal: a node's run *taken on its own*, with an old move that is not refuted,\ncan refute a solvable state and record it. One isolated customer with $k = 1$\nand the old move $Q = \\{0\\}$ is enough (Lean: `exec_fake_oldMove`). But the\nhypothesis of Theorem 4.18 holds in every real run, because every old move is a\nrefutation completed earlier in the same run. So the combination is sound,\nwhich is what the C has always run and what every production refutation used.\nWhat is lost is that a memo entry cannot be checked without re-deriving the\ncited subtree's $Q$, which is why the certificate checker declines that\nconfiguration.\n\n### 4.4.3 The theorem\n\n**Theorem 4.19 (the revised search is sound).** Run the customer search with\nfree moves, the memo, old move and the filter\n$\\text{definite} \\to \\text{subset} \\to \\text{better}$, with the definite move\nfiring only on a hereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only\nunder the repaired premise, with any limit $L$, any child order and any memo\npolicy. If the run refutes $k$ from the root, then:\n\n| conclusion | Lean |\n|---|---|\n| no closing order costs at most $k$, $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_sound` |\n| $k < \\nu(G)$, the narrowness | `exec_repairedFullFilter_narrowness` |\n| $\\mathrm{pw}(G) > k - 1$, for nonempty $C$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth` |\n| on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a requirement, $Z(M) > k$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue` |\n| the same in pathwidth form, $\\mathrm{pw}(G_M) > k - 1$ | `exec_repairedFullFilter_mospGraph_pathwidth` |\n\n*Proof.* Proposition 4.17 makes the filter node-sound, and it returns only\nplayable customers. At the root the invariant holds, the memo is empty and\n$Q = \\emptyset$, so Theorem 4.18 gives $\\neg\\mathrm{Sol}_k(\\emptyset)$. Lemma 4.2\nat the root turns this into $\\neg P_k(\\emptyset)$, and Theorem 4.3 turns it into\n$\\mathrm{pw}(G) + 1 > k$ and, on $G_M$, $Z(M) > k$. $\\square$\n\n*In plain English.* Each rule throws away only moves that some kept move is\nproved to be at least as good as. The memo and the old-move list only ever\nremember failures that really happened earlier in the same search. So when the\nsearch says \"no\", every way of finishing was either tried or provably no better\nthan one that was tried, and no closing order fits in $k$ stacks. By the\nearlier sections, that means the instance needs more than $k$ stacks and its\ngraph has pathwidth at least $k$.\n\n### 4.4.4 What holds for the code as it stands\n\nThe code runs the unrepaired premises: $\\mathrm{close} \\ge \\mathrm{open}$ for\nthe definite move, and premises 3 and 4 for the better move. Its filter is\n**not** node-sound. On the graph of Counterexample 4.5 at $k = 6$, for every\n$L$, it keeps only $0$ at the state $\\{2\\}$, which has a solution, while\n$\\mathrm{cl}(\\{0, 2\\})$ has none (Lean: `codeFullFilter_cex`). Against the named\nproperty `CodeFilterSound` (the code's filter is node-sound at every state of\none instance at one $k$), this is `not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph`.\n\nWhat holds is conditional, and the condition is named rather than assumed.\n\n- **The gap, named.** `CodeRunSound` says that the code's run expands only\n  nodes at which its filter keeps a solution whenever one exists. It is a\n  hypothesis, not a theorem. Under it, a code refutation is genuine and gives\n  $Z(M) > k$ (Lean: `ExecOn`, `ExecOn.sound`, `codeExec_sound_of_runSound`,\n  `codeExec_pathwidth_of_runSound`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_runSound`).\n- **A checkable condition at one node.** `CodeNodeRepaired` holds at a node\n  when, if the definite move fires, its pick is hereditarily definite, and, if\n  it does not fire, every customer the better move drops has *some* earlier\n  cited survivor meeting the repaired premise. It implies node soundness\n  (Lean: `nodeSoundAt_codeFullFilter_of_repaired`). A run of the code whose\n  every expanded node passes it is sound and gives $Z(M) > k$ (Lean:\n  `codeExec_sound_of_repaired`, `codeExec_mospValue_of_repaired`). This is what\n  a certificate would have to carry to certify a refutation by the unrepaired\n  code.\n\n**Why a false refutation is hard to produce.** Every node the search expands is\nreached from the root by playable moves. If the root has no solution, no\nexpanded node has one, and every expanded node is node-sound for free. So a\nfalse refutation needs a $k$ at which a solution exists, together with a lost\nnode on every branch that leads to a solution.\n\n**Lost nodes do occur in real runs.** In the check of section 4.5, the code's\nown run reaches a node where its filter loses the last solution in 58 of\n570,206 runs. All 58 are on graphs built around the definite-move\ncounterexample (29 graphs, each at two values of $L$), all at\n$k = \\mathrm{opt}$ and never at $\\mathrm{opt} - 1$, as the previous paragraph\nrequires. The smallest has 14 customers. Every one of the 58 runs found a\nsolution through another branch and answered correctly. In 26 of them the lost\nsubtree joined `seen` and passed a non-refuted old move to its siblings, at 83\nnodes in all, which is the cascade Theorem 4.18's hypothesis excludes. It did\nnot change an answer. Over the same runs, `CodeNodeRepaired` held at\n1,781,000 of 1,782,100 expanded nodes and failed at 1,100, most of which still\nkept a solution.\n\n**The certificate checker.** The search certificate\n(`learning/search_certificate.py`) checks each pruning step against a premise.\nFor the free moves, the memo, the subset rule, the covering condition and the\nold moves, the premise it checks is the one the Lean lemma needs. For the\ndefinite and better steps it checks the code's premises, which\nCounterexamples 4.5 and 4.13 show can certify a false link. A certificate whose\ndefinite and better steps meet the repaired premises proves $Z(M) > k$ with no\ntrust in the search, by `nodeSoundAt_of_covering` at each node, then\n`ExecOn.sound`, then Theorem 4.19's chain. That the checker's Python computes\nthe quantities named is a reading of the code, backed by tests, and not proved.\n\n**Open.** Whether the unrepaired code ever answers *unsat* wrongly on a whole\ninstance. None is known.\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 The evidence\n\nEvery statement above was checked by brute force before it was proved.\n`paper2/search_check.py` implements the model and the rules from the written\nstatements, sharing no code with the solver, and compares them with an exact\noracle for $\\mathrm{Sol}_k$ computed over all states. The first check (item 06)\ncovered every labelled graph on 1 to 6 vertices (33,867 graphs), every graph on\n7 vertices under the identity and 24 random labellings (26,100), 6,000 random\ngraphs on 8 to 16 vertices, and three pinned instances, at every $k$. For the\nrules as coded it found zero failures of node soundness, of cited links, of the\ncovering condition and of whole-search answers, over 111,288,536, 90,915,044\nand 355,181,036 node checks in the three families. It also confirmed that both\npre-fix forms of the better move fail: Bug A's conclusion first fails at 12\nvertices, and Bug B first loses a node at 8. The same runs went through the C\nsolver, and **the port matched the C in answer and node count on all 4,418,084\nruns**, including the faulty variants. That is what shows the rules of section\n4.3 are the rules the C runs.\n\nThat check did not see Theorem 1's fault. The smallest graph on which the\ndefinite move loses the last solution has 14 customers. There is none on any\ngraph to 9 vertices (exhaustive) and none on the 88,802 connected graphs on 10\nvertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was found by a hunt in a\n\"gadget\" family built from the failed proof, and minimised by deletion to 14\ncustomers and 26 edges, never fewer. Under the repair and its matching form the\nhunt found zero losses in 2.05 G premise checks. A port of the search with the\ncode's definite move gave no false refutation at the optimum on 600,000 random\ngraphs at 10 to 18 vertices and 90,000 gadget graphs at 12 to 17. The final\ncheck (item 12) ran 570,206 searches with memo and old move: the repaired\nsearch agreed with the oracle on every one, and the code gave zero wrong\nanswers. Each later item has its own check table in `search_soundness.md`\n§4.1.4 to §5.4, all with zero failures for the proved statements.\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 What changes in practice\n\n### 4.6.1 The revised algorithm\n\nThe search is unchanged except for two tests. Below, $\\mathrm{MaxMatch}(S, q)$\nis the size of a largest matching between the customers\n$Y = \\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{q\\}) \\setminus (S \\cup \\{q\\})$ and the stacks\n$o(q, S)$, joining $d$ to the stacks of $o(d, S)$.\n\n```\nfilter(S, Q):                                   # S a state, Q the old moves\n  P := [c not in S ∪ Q : cost(S, c) ≤ k], in index order\n  for q in P:                                   # definite move\n    if open(q, S) ≤ close(q, S)                 #   the published test\n       and MaxMatch(S, q) ≥ open(q, S) − 1:     #   the repair (Theorem 4.8)\n      return [q]\n  W := [r in P : no d in C∖S dominates r]       # subset rule, index tie-break\n  if W is empty: W := P                         #   fallback\n  kept := []\n  for r in W, in index order:                   # better move\n    T := cl(S ∪ {r})\n    drop := exists an earlier q in W (among the first L if L > 0) with\n              |(O(T) ∪ N[q]) ∖ (S ∪ {r})| ≤ k   #   premise 3\n              and open(q, T) ≤ close(q, T)      #   premise 4, Theorem 2's test\n              and MaxMatch(T, q) ≥ open(q, T) − 1   # the repair at the child\n    if not drop: append r to kept\n  return kept\n```\n\nThe free moves, the memo, the old moves with their reinsertion test, the cost\ncut and the child order are as before. Keeping the published test in front of\nthe matching is only a shortcut, since the repair implies it.\n\n### 4.6.2 Its cost\n\nThe repair adds one bipartite matching per candidate that passes the published\ntest, and nothing for the others. The matching is small: it joins at most\n$\\mathrm{close} - 1$ customers to $\\mathrm{open}$ stacks, and it can stop as\nsoon as it has $\\mathrm{open} - 1$ edges. Every candidate with\n$\\mathrm{open} \\le 1$ passes at once (Corollary to Theorem 4.7). In the better\nmove the same matching is computed at the child $\\mathrm{cl}(S \\cup \\{r\\})$,\nonce per pair $(r, q)$ that passes premises 3 and 4. By Theorem 4.8 the test is\nexact: where it fails, the repaired premise fails, and a solution may avoid\n$q$. So no sound pruning that the repaired theorems allow is given up. How\noften the published test fires without the repair is measured on small graphs\nonly (20,857 times in 23,978,489 firings, `search_soundness.md` §4.2.4), and\nthe effect on node counts at scale is not yet measured.\n\n### 4.6.3 What has to be redone\n\n- **The solvers.** The definite move and the better move change in the C\n  customer search, in its Python reference, and in the graph pathwidth solver\n  (`../pathwidth_solver/`), as the owner decided.\n- **Re-certification.** Every value in the corpus whose optimality rests only\n  on a customer-search refutation is re-certified with the revised search.\n  Values certified by a checked DRAT refutation through the SAT path, or by a\n  lower bound equal to the value, do not depend on the search and stand as\n  they are. No value is known to be wrong.\n- **The certificate route.** The certificate checker's definite and better\n  steps must check the repaired premises: one subset enumeration or one\n  matching per step. With that change, an accepted certificate proves\n  $Z(M) > k$ by Theorem 4.19's chain without trusting the search, which is the\n  route this paper's dataset relies on for values the SAT path cannot\n  reach.\n","numLines":979,"startLine":1,"totalLines":979}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UeCYJKqxUk4mF64Pqp79qr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n2\t\n3\tAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\n4\tspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\n5\tstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\n6\tthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\n7\tbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n8\t\n9\tWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\n10\tsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\n11\tagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\n12\tThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\n13\topen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\n14\tslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\n15\ttheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n16\t\n17\t**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\n18\tthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n19\t`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\n20\tbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\n21\tcloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\n22\tmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n23\t\n24\t**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\n25\tcosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\n26\ttheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\n27\tcustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\n28\tthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\n29\twrong.\n30\t\n31\t**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\n32\tbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n33\t\n34\t- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n35\t  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n36\t  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n37\t- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n38\t  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n39\t  so **all** other branches go.\n40\t- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n41\t  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n42\t  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n43\t- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n44\t  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n45\t  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, implemented in the C only, and **worth it only\n46\t  on sparse instances** -- see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.\n47\t\n48\t**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n49\t\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\n50\tPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\n51\tbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\n52\tan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\n53\tand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\n54\twould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\n55\tin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n56\t\"\"\"\n57\t\n58\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n59\t\n60\timport time\n61\tfrom collections.abc import Callable\n62\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n63\t\n64\tfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\n65\tfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\n66\tfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n67\t    FAN_ORDERS,\n68\t    _neighbour_masks,\n69\t    fan_sort_key,\n70\t    product_order_from_customers,\n71\t)\n72\t\n73\t\n74\t@dataclass\n75\tclass Decision:\n76\t    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n77\t\n78\t    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n79\t    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n80\t    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n81\t    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n82\t    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n83\t    \"\"\"\n84\t\n85\t    status: str\n86\t    order: list[int] | None\n87\t    nodes: int\n88\t\n89\t\n90\t# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n91\t# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n92\t# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n93\t#\n94\t#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n95\t#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n96\t#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n97\t#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n98\t#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n99\t#\n100\t# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n101\t# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n102\t# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n103\t# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\n104\tBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n105\t\n106\t\n107\tdef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n108\t    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n109\t\n110\t    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n111\t    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n112\t    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n113\t    a \"-8-\").\n114\t    \"\"\"\n115\t    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n116\t        return False\n117\t    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n118\t\n119\t\n120\tdef decide(\n121\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n122\t    k: int,\n123\t    *,\n124\t    restrict: bool = False,\n125\t    subset_rule: bool = True,\n126\t    definite_move: bool = True,\n127\t    old_move: bool = True,\n128\t    memo: bool = True,\n129\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n130\t    deadline: float | None = None,\n131\t    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n132\t    native: bool = True,\n133\t    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n134\t    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n135\t    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n136\t    **kwargs: object,\n137\t) -> Decision:\n138\t    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n139\t\n140\t    Args:\n141\t        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n142\t        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n143\t            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n144\t            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n145\t        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n146\t            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n147\t            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n148\t        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n149\t            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n150\t            failure by whatever path reached it.\n151\t        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n152\t        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n153\t            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n154\t            budget actually has.\n155\t        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n156\t            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n157\t            refutation will take the machine down.\n158\t        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n159\t            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n160\t            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n161\t            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n162\t            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n163\t        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n164\t            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n165\t            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n166\t            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n167\t            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n168\t            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n169\t            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n170\t            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n171\t            writing.\n172\t        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n173\t            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n174\t            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n175\t            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n176\t            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n177\t            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n178\t            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n179\t            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n180\t            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n181\t            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n182\t        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n183\t            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n184\t            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n185\t            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n186\t            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n187\t            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n188\t            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n189\t            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n190\t            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n191\t\n192\t    Returns:\n193\t        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n194\t        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n195\t    \"\"\"\n196\t    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n197\t        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n198\t\n199\t    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n200\t        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n201\t        answer = decide_native(\n202\t            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n203\t            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n204\t            better_move=kwargs.pop(\"better_move\", False),\n205\t            better_move_dominators=kwargs.pop(\"better_move_dominators\", 4),\n206\t            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n207\t            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n208\t            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n209\t            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n210\t            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n211\t            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n212\t            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n213\t            fan_order=fan_order)\n214\t        if answer is not None:\n215\t            return answer\n216\t\n217\t    if old_move and memo:\n218\t        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n219\t        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n220\t        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n221\t        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n222\t        memo = False\n223\t\n224\t    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n225\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n226\t\n227\t    full = 0\n228\t    for customer in active:\n229\t        full |= 1 << customer\n230\t\n231\t    if not active:\n232\t        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n233\t\n234\t    path: list[int] = []\n235\t    refuted: set[int] = set()\n236\t    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n237\t\n238\t    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n239\t        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n240\t\n241\t        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n242\t        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n243\t        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n244\t        \"\"\"\n245\t        found = 0\n246\t        bits = full & ~closed\n247\t        while bits:\n248\t            bit = bits & -bits\n249\t            bits ^= bit\n250\t            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n251\t                found |= bit\n252\t        return found\n253\t\n254\t    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n255\t        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n256\t        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n257\t        mark = len(path)\n258\t\n259\t        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n260\t        if free:\n261\t            bits = free\n262\t            while bits:\n263\t                bit = bits & -bits\n264\t                bits ^= bit\n265\t                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n266\t            closed |= free\n267\t\n268\t        if closed == full:\n269\t            return True\n270\t        if memo and closed in refuted:\n271\t            del path[mark:]\n272\t            return False\n273\t\n274\t        remaining = full & ~closed\n275\t        seen &= remaining\n276\t        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n277\t        if restrict:\n278\t            narrowed = remaining & opened\n279\t            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n280\t            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n281\t            if narrowed:\n282\t                candidates = narrowed\n283\t\n284\t        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n285\t        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n286\t        bits = remaining\n287\t        while bits:\n288\t            bit = bits & -bits\n289\t            bits ^= bit\n290\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n291\t            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n292\t\n293\t        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n294\t        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n295\t        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n296\t        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n297\t        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n298\t        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n299\t        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n300\t        # stays sound.\n301\t        if expansion_prune and opens:\n302\t            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n303\t            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n304\t            floor = 0\n305\t            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n306\t                need = size - step + 1\n307\t                if need > floor:\n308\t                    floor = need\n309\t            if open_now + floor > k:\n310\t                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n311\t                    refuted.add(closed)\n312\t                del path[mark:]\n313\t                return False\n314\t\n315\t        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n316\t        bits = candidates\n317\t        while bits:\n318\t            bit = bits & -bits\n319\t            bits ^= bit\n320\t            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n321\t            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n322\t            if cost <= k:\n323\t                playable.append((cost, customer))\n324\t\n325\t        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move):\n326\t            playable = _apply_dominance(\n327\t                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move)\n328\t\n329\t        if fan_order == \"index\":\n330\t            playable.sort()\n331\t        else:\n332\t            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n333\t        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n334\t            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n335\t        for _, customer in playable:\n336\t            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n337\t            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n338\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n339\t                del path[mark:]\n340\t                return False\n341\t            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n342\t                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n343\t                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n344\t                del path[mark:]\n345\t                return False\n346\t\n347\t            here = len(path)\n348\t            path.append(customer)\n349\t            inherited = 0\n350\t            if old_move and seen:\n351\t                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n352\t                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n353\t                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n354\t                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n355\t                bits = seen\n356\t                while bits:\n357\t                    bit = bits & -bits\n358\t                    bits ^= bit\n359\t                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n360\t                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n361\t                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n362\t                    if cost <= k:\n363\t                        inherited |= bit\n364\t            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n365\t                      inherited):\n366\t                return True\n367\t            del path[here:]\n368\t            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n369\t                del path[mark:]\n370\t                return False\n371\t            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n372\t            # instead would be repeating it.\n373\t            seen |= 1 << customer\n374\t\n375\t        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n376\t        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n377\t        # permanent wrong answer.\n378\t        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n379\t            refuted.add(closed)\n380\t        del path[mark:]\n381\t        return False\n382\t\n383\t    found = search(0, 0)\n384\t    if found:\n385\t        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n386\t    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n387\t        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n388\t    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n389\t\n390\t\n391\tdef _apply_dominance(\n392\t    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n393\t    opens: dict[int, int],\n394\t    subset_rule: bool,\n395\t    definite_move: bool,\n396\t) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n397\t    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the two dominance relations.\n398\t\n399\t    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n400\t    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n401\t    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n402\t    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n403\t    \"\"\"\n404\t    if definite_move:\n405\t        for cost, q in playable:\n406\t            own = opens[q]\n407\t            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n408\t            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n409\t            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n410\t                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n411\t                # branch can go.\n412\t                return [(cost, q)]\n413\t\n414\t    if not subset_rule:\n415\t        return playable\n416\t\n417\t    kept = []\n418\t    for cost, q in playable:\n419\t        own = opens[q]\n420\t        dominated = any(\n421\t            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n422\t            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n423\t        )\n424\t        if not dominated:\n425\t            kept.append((cost, q))\n426\t    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n427\t    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n428\t    return kept or playable\n429\t\n430\t\n431\t@dataclass\n432\tclass Solution:\n433\t    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n434\t\n435\t    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n436\t    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n437\t    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n438\t    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n439\t    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n440\t\n441\t    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n442\t    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n443\t    \"\"\"\n444\t\n445\t    value: int\n446\t    order: list[int]\n447\t    proof: str\n448\t    nodes: int\n449\t    seconds: float\n450\t\n451\t    @property\n452\t    def proved(self) -> bool:\n453\t        return bool(self.proof)\n454\t\n455\t\n456\tdef solve(\n457\t    instance: MOSPInstance,\n458\t    *,\n459\t    upper: int | None = None,\n460\t    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n461\t    lower: int = 0,\n462\t    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n463\t    time_budget: float | None = None,\n464\t    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n465\t    **kwargs: object,\n466\t) -> Solution:\n467\t    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n468\t\n469\t    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n470\t    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n471\t    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n472\t    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n473\t    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n474\t\n475\t    Args:\n476\t        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n477\t        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n478\t            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n479\t        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n480\t            descent with `proved` false.\n481\t        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n482\t            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n483\t            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n484\t            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n485\t            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n486\t            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n487\t            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n488\t        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n489\t            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n490\t            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n491\t            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n492\t            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n493\t\n494\t    Returns:\n495\t        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n496\t        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n497\t    \"\"\"\n498\t    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n499\t\n500\t    started = time.monotonic()\n501\t    nodes = 0\n502\t\n503\t    if upper is None:\n504\t        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n505\t        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n506\t    else:\n507\t        value = upper\n508\t        order = []\n509\t\n510\t    if value <= lower:\n511\t        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n512\t\n513\t    k = value - 1\n514\t    while k >= lower:\n515\t        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n516\t        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n517\t        nodes += answer.nodes\n518\t\n519\t        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n520\t            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n521\t            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n522\t            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n523\t            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n524\t            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n525\t            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n526\t            # project has had to retract before.\n527\t            if value == k + 1:\n528\t                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n529\t                                time.monotonic() - started)\n530\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n531\t        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n532\t            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n533\t\n534\t        found = answer.order\n535\t        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n536\t            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n537\t        if achieved < value:\n538\t            value, order = achieved, found\n539\t            if on_improve is not None:\n540\t                on_improve(value, order)\n541\t        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n542\t\n543\t    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n544\t    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n545\t    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n546\t\n547\t\n548\tdef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n549\t    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n550\t    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n551\t    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n552\t    return sorted(\n553\t        active,\n554\t        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n555\t    )\n556\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4b6cad2f-824e-4666-8c2e-24a676325537","uuid":"d449feff-c20b-43f0-bf80-1164f161d780","timestamp":"2026-10-01T13:11:13.437Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/satisfiability/customer_search.py","content":"\"\"\"Complete search over customer closing orders (Chu & Stuckey 2009, §2-3).\n\nAn alternative to the SAT encoding for deciding \"MOSP(I) <= k?\". It searches the\nspace the plan's §0.1 established is the right one -- orders in which customers'\nstacks *close* -- rather than the space of product sequences, and prunes it with\nthe dominance relations of their §3, none of which can be written as clauses\nbecause every one conditions on the partial sequence already committed.\n\nWhy this exists alongside the SAT path: their Table 1 reports the complete\nsearch closing 125-125-6, 125-125-8 and 125-125-10 in 9 s, 0.19 s and 0.02 s,\nagainst our SAT refutations, which do not return on those instances at all.\nThe densities where their search is fastest are the ones holding most of our\nopen gap. It is also the reverse of the SAT path's profile -- their search is\nslowest exactly where sparsity makes the branching factor explode, which is what\ntheir relaxation technique (plan item 5) exists to repair.\n\n**The measure.** A state is the set `S` of customers already closed. `O(S)` is\nthe set opened so far, the union of the neighbourhoods `N(c)` over `c ∈ S`, with\n`N` self-inclusive. Closing `c` next costs `|O(S ∪ {c}) - S|`: the stacks opened\nbut not yet closed, counting `c` itself, which is still open at the moment it\ncloses. `min` over closing orders of the peak of this equals the true optimum --\nmeasured 400/400 against exhaustive search, `reports/chu_stuckey_plan.md` §0.1.\n\n**Free moves.** A remaining customer whose whole neighbourhood is already opened\ncosts nothing to close and is closed immediately. This is the `open = 0` case of\ntheir Theorem 1 and it is what keeps the measure above honest: without it, a\ncustomer whose products are all made would keep counting as an open stack, and\nthe \"close\" counts the dominance rules below are stated in terms of would be\nwrong.\n\n**The rules.** Each is a dominance: it discards branches that cannot beat the\nbranches kept, so a refutation still refutes.\n\n- *subset* (their §2): if `o(cᵢ,S) ⊆ o(cⱼ,S)` then closing `cᵢ` first is never\n  worse, since closing `cⱼ` opens everything `cᵢ` would have and closing `cᵢ`\n  can only help `cⱼ` -- so `cⱼ` leaves the candidate set.\n- *definite move* (their Theorem 1): if `close(q,S) ≥ open(q,S)` and `S ++ [q]`\n  is playable, then every solution extending `S` has one extending `S ++ [q]`,\n  so **all** other branches go.\n- *old move* (their Theorem 3): if `q` was already searched at an ancestor and\n  inserting `q` back there leaves the sequence playable, that subtree has been\n  seen and `q` goes. Maintained as a set `Q(S)` in `O(|C|)` per node.\n- *better move* (their Theorem 2): if `S ++ [q]` and `S ++ [r, q]` are playable\n  and `close(q, S ∪ {r}) ≥ open(q, S ∪ {r})`, then `r` goes. `O(|R|³)` per node\n  against Theorem 1's `O(|R|²)`, implemented in the C only, and **worth it only\n  on sparse instances** -- see `sparse_enough_for_better_move`.\n\n**Old move and the memo, together.** Chu & Stuckey run both -- \"better move\",\n\"old move\" and nogood recording on at once -- and the C follows them. The\nPython still refuses the combination, because the argument against it has not\nbeen retired: a failure reached with old-move pruning depends on which branches\nan *ancestor* had searched, so it is a property of the path rather than of `S`,\nand recording it against `S` alone could refute a state that some other path\nwould not. Whether that can actually happen is settled by the exhaustive tests\nin `tests/test_native.py`, not by the argument.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport time\nfrom collections.abc import Callable\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nfrom mosp.instance import MOSPInstance\nfrom mosp.verify import max_open_stacks\nfrom satisfiability.heuristics import (\n    FAN_ORDERS,\n    _neighbour_masks,\n    fan_sort_key,\n    product_order_from_customers,\n)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Decision:\n    \"\"\"The answer to \"MOSP(I) <= k?\", and what it cost to get it.\n\n    `status` is \"sat\" with a closing order, \"unsat\" -- a genuine refutation, the\n    whole space having been searched -- or \"unknown\", which means the node\n    budget ran out and nothing was proved either way. A restricted search never\n    returns \"unsat\": the restriction discards branches it cannot justify, so\n    exhausting what remains proves nothing.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    status: str\n    order: list[int] | None\n    nodes: int\n\n\n# Products per customer below which Theorem 2 pays. Measured on refutations\n# whose answers were already known, counting branch decisions, which is the one\n# figure a loaded machine cannot distort:\n#\n#   products/customer   nodes without   with Theorem 2   verdict\n#   2.6 (open instance)   263,000,000+        885,759    closed it, in 3s\n#   2.3                     7,327,112         55,424     15x faster\n#   6.1                    13,300,731      7,825,793     0.8x, a slight loss\n#   8.0                    11,902,214      8,652,247     0.45x, a clear loss\n#\n# The rule inverts with density because the theorem ranges over pairs of\n# candidates: sparse instances keep many candidates playable at each step, which\n# is exactly where pruning a pair at a time repays its cubic cost. Dense ones\n# run out of candidates quickly and the cost is wasted.\nBETTER_MOVE_DENSITY = 5.0\n\n\ndef sparse_enough_for_better_move(instance: MOSPInstance) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether Theorem 2 should be used on this instance.\n\n    Chosen by measurement rather than by the instance's name: the Chu & Stuckey\n    generator's density parameter is not recorded in the file, and the ratio\n    below reproduces it closely enough (2.57 for a \"-2-\" instance, 8.01 for\n    a \"-8-\").\n    \"\"\"\n    if instance.n_customers == 0:\n        return False\n    return float(instance.matrix.sum()) / instance.n_customers <= BETTER_MOVE_DENSITY\n\n\ndef decide(\n    instance: MOSPInstance,\n    k: int,\n    *,\n    restrict: bool = False,\n    subset_rule: bool = True,\n    definite_move: bool = True,\n    old_move: bool = True,\n    memo: bool = True,\n    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n    deadline: float | None = None,\n    memo_limit: int = 4_000_000,\n    native: bool = True,\n    branch: \"Callable[[int, int, list[tuple[int, int]]], list[tuple[int, int]]] | None\" = None,\n    expansion_prune: bool = False,\n    fan_order: str = \"index\",\n    **kwargs: object,\n) -> Decision:\n    \"\"\"Decide \"MOSP(instance) <= k?\" by searching customer closing orders.\n\n    Args:\n        k: the budget on simultaneously open stacks.\n        restrict: branch only on `R ∩ O(S)`, customers already open. This is\n            their `ub_MOSP` (§3.4) and is **not sound** -- it answers \"sat\" or\n            \"unknown\", never \"unsat\".\n        subset_rule, definite_move, old_move: the dominance relations above. The\n            flags exist so the exhaustive tests can vary one at a time.\n            `old_move` cannot be combined with `memo`, for the reason above.\n        memo: record states already refuted, their `prob[S]`. The state is\n            `S` alone -- `O(S)` is a function of it -- so a failure at `S` is a\n            failure by whatever path reached it.\n        max_nodes: abort after this many branches and return \"unknown\".\n        deadline: `time.monotonic()` value to stop at, checked every 4,096\n            nodes. Same effect as `max_nodes`, in the unit a caller with a\n            budget actually has.\n        memo_limit: stop growing the memo past this many states. The table is\n            what bounds memory here, and an unbounded one on a 125-customer\n            refutation will take the machine down.\n        native: use the C port when it applies. It visits the same nodes in the\n            same order -- the counts match exactly on every instance measured --\n            about 120x faster. Set False to force the Python, which is the\n            reference implementation and what the exhaustive tests are written\n            against. `satisfiability/native.py` says when the C declines.\n        expansion_prune: cut a whole node when even the best continuation must\n            exceed `k`, by the neighbourhood-expansion argument applied to the\n            customers still remaining rather than to the instance as a whole\n            (`reports/expansion_bound.md`). The rule generalises the cost cut\n            this search already makes: that cut is the one-step case, and every\n            further step is new pruning. Costs one pass over data the node has\n            already computed. **Forces the Python path** -- the C does not\n            implement it -- so it is for measuring whether a port is worth\n            writing.\n        branch: reorder the surviving candidates at each node. Called with\n            `(closed, opened, playable)` where `playable` is the `(cost,\n            customer)` list the dominance rules left, and must return a\n            permutation of it -- dropping a candidate would make a refutation\n            unsound, and the caller is trusted not to. Default is cheapest\n            first. **Forces the Python path**: the C has no way to call back,\n            so this is for measuring whether a different order is worth porting,\n            not for production. Order affects only *which* branches are visited\n            first: the cost cut, the memo and every dominance rule are\n            properties of the state, not of the order they are reached in.\n        fan_order: how equal-cost candidates are ordered. `\"index\"` (the\n            default, and what every recorded node count was made with) breaks\n            ties by customer index; `\"degree\"` puts the candidate with the\n            most neighbours not yet closed first, then index -- the fan order\n            of the two-key rule in `reports/ml_nature.md` §7. Implemented in\n            both the C and the Python, so it costs nothing to ask for. Like\n            `branch`, it changes which branches are visited first and never\n            which are visited, so the answer is the same under either; the\n            node count is what it is measured on (plan 2 §2.5a).\n\n    Returns:\n        A `Decision`. The \"sat\" order closes every customer with a non-empty\n        product set; customers needing nothing are omitted, as they never open.\n    \"\"\"\n    if fan_order not in FAN_ORDERS:\n        raise ValueError(f\"fan_order must be one of {FAN_ORDERS}, not {fan_order!r}\")\n\n    if native and branch is None and not expansion_prune:\n        from satisfiability.native import decide_native\n        answer = decide_native(\n            instance, k, restrict=restrict, subset_rule=subset_rule,\n            definite_move=definite_move, old_move=old_move, memo=memo,\n            better_move=kwargs.pop(\"better_move\", False),\n            better_move_dominators=kwargs.pop(\"better_move_dominators\", 4),\n            # Reverts of the 2026-09-26 fix, unsound, for measurement only\n            # (learning.fix_cost); meaningless without `better_move`.\n            old_close_count=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_close_count\", False)),\n            old_rule_order=bool(kwargs.pop(\"old_rule_order\", False)),\n            subset_after_better_move=bool(kwargs.pop(\"subset_after_better_move\", False)),\n            max_nodes=max_nodes, memo_limit=memo_limit,\n            seconds=None if deadline is None else max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic()),\n            fan_order=fan_order)\n        if answer is not None:\n            return answer\n\n    if old_move and memo:\n        # The C runs both, as Chu & Stuckey do, and 10,476 exhaustive decisions\n        # agree with brute force. This Python reference keeps the stricter\n        # reading -- the memo is dropped rather than combined -- so that the two\n        # implementations are not both resting on the same assumption.\n        memo = False\n\n    masks = _neighbour_masks(instance)\n    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n\n    full = 0\n    for customer in active:\n        full |= 1 << customer\n\n    if not active:\n        return Decision(\"sat\", [], 0)\n\n    path: list[int] = []\n    refuted: set[int] = set()\n    state = {\"nodes\": 0, \"aborted\": False}\n\n    def free_moves(closed: int, opened: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Customers whose neighbourhood is wholly opened: free to close now.\n\n        Closing one opens nothing, so the peak cannot rise, and it can only\n        release later steps. Closing them does not change `O`, so no further\n        free moves appear and one pass suffices.\n        \"\"\"\n        found = 0\n        bits = full & ~closed\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            if masks[bit.bit_length() - 1] & ~opened == 0:\n                found |= bit\n        return found\n\n    def search(closed: int, opened: int, seen: int = 0) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"`seen` is Q(S): moves an ancestor already searched that could be\n        played here instead, without making the sequence unplayable.\"\"\"\n        mark = len(path)\n\n        free = free_moves(closed, opened)\n        if free:\n            bits = free\n            while bits:\n                bit = bits & -bits\n                bits ^= bit\n                path.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)\n            closed |= free\n\n        if closed == full:\n            return True\n        if memo and closed in refuted:\n            del path[mark:]\n            return False\n\n        remaining = full & ~closed\n        seen &= remaining\n        candidates = remaining & ~seen if old_move else remaining\n        if restrict:\n            narrowed = remaining & opened\n            # An empty frontier means a disconnected remainder, where some\n            # stack has to be opened before anything can close.\n            if narrowed:\n                candidates = narrowed\n\n        # o(c,S) = N(c) - O(S), the stacks closing c would newly open.\n        opens: dict[int, int] = {}\n        bits = remaining\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n            opens[customer] = masks[customer] & ~opened\n\n        # Expansion cut. Closing any `t` of the remaining customers costs at\n        # least `open_now + m_(t) - t + 1`, where `m_(t)` is the t-th smallest\n        # number of stacks a single remaining customer would newly open: any\n        # `t` distinct customers include one whose own count is at least that.\n        # `t = 1` is the cost cut below; `t >= 2` is what this adds. The value\n        # depends only on `closed` -- `opened` is a function of it -- so a state\n        # refuted here is refuted by whatever path reached it, and the memo\n        # stays sound.\n        if expansion_prune and opens:\n            open_now = (opened & ~closed).bit_count()\n            sizes = sorted(mask.bit_count() for mask in opens.values())\n            floor = 0\n            for step, size in enumerate(sizes, start=1):\n                need = size - step + 1\n                if need > floor:\n                    floor = need\n            if open_now + floor > k:\n                if memo and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n                    refuted.add(closed)\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n\n        playable: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n        bits = candidates\n        while bits:\n            bit = bits & -bits\n            bits ^= bit\n            customer = bit.bit_length() - 1\n            cost = ((opened | masks[customer]) & ~closed).bit_count()\n            if cost <= k:\n                playable.append((cost, customer))\n\n        if playable and (subset_rule or definite_move):\n            playable = _apply_dominance(\n                playable, opens, subset_rule, definite_move)\n\n        if fan_order == \"index\":\n            playable.sort()\n        else:\n            playable.sort(key=fan_sort_key(fan_order, masks, remaining))\n        if branch is not None and len(playable) > 1:\n            playable = branch(closed, opened, playable)\n        for _, customer in playable:\n            state[\"nodes\"] += 1\n            if max_nodes is not None and state[\"nodes\"] > max_nodes:\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            if (deadline is not None and state[\"nodes\"] % 4096 == 0\n                    and time.monotonic() > deadline):\n                state[\"aborted\"] = True\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n\n            here = len(path)\n            path.append(customer)\n            inherited = 0\n            if old_move and seen:\n                # Q(S ++ [c]) keeps those q whose reinsertion still leaves this\n                # last move playable; everything earlier is playable already by\n                # q being in Q(S). Auto-closures can only lower that cost, so\n                # checking the move alone is conservative in the safe direction.\n                bits = seen\n                while bits:\n                    bit = bits & -bits\n                    bits ^= bit\n                    other = bit.bit_length() - 1\n                    cost = ((opened | masks[other] | masks[customer])\n                            & ~(closed | bit)).bit_count()\n                    if cost <= k:\n                        inherited |= bit\n            if search(closed | (1 << customer), opened | masks[customer],\n                      inherited):\n                return True\n            del path[here:]\n            if state[\"aborted\"]:\n                del path[mark:]\n                return False\n            # This branch is now searched, so a later sibling that could play it\n            # instead would be repeating it.\n            seen |= 1 << customer\n\n        # Only a genuine exhaustion may be recorded: a budget abort has not\n        # refuted anything, and memoising it would turn a timeout into a\n        # permanent wrong answer.\n        if memo and not state[\"aborted\"] and len(refuted) < memo_limit:\n            refuted.add(closed)\n        del path[mark:]\n        return False\n\n    found = search(0, 0)\n    if found:\n        return Decision(\"sat\", list(path), state[\"nodes\"])\n    if state[\"aborted\"] or restrict:\n        return Decision(\"unknown\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n    return Decision(\"unsat\", None, state[\"nodes\"])\n\n\ndef _apply_dominance(\n    playable: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    opens: dict[int, int],\n    subset_rule: bool,\n    definite_move: bool,\n) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"Cut the candidate list by the two dominance relations.\n\n    `close(q,S) = |{d ∉ S : o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)}|` counts the stacks that closing\n    `q` releases: `d` is finished once every stack it touches has been opened,\n    which after `q` means `o(d,S) ⊆ o(q,S)`. Both `close` and the subset test\n    fall out of the same pass over the remaining customers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if definite_move:\n        for cost, q in playable:\n            own = opens[q]\n            opened_by_q = own.bit_count()\n            closed_by_q = sum(1 for other in opens.values() if other & ~own == 0)\n            if closed_by_q >= opened_by_q:\n                # q is at least as good as anything else here, so every other\n                # branch can go.\n                return [(cost, q)]\n\n    if not subset_rule:\n        return playable\n\n    kept = []\n    for cost, q in playable:\n        own = opens[q]\n        dominated = any(\n            other & ~own == 0 and (other != own or d < q)\n            for d, other in opens.items() if d != q\n        )\n        if not dominated:\n            kept.append((cost, q))\n    # Every candidate dominated by a non-candidate (possible only under the\n    # frontier restriction) would empty the list; keep the original in that case.\n    return kept or playable\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Solution:\n    \"\"\"What a descent established, and how -- which is not the same question.\n\n    `proof` is \"refutation\" when the search exhausted `value - 1`, \"bound\" when\n    `value` met a lower bound that needed no refutation, and empty when the\n    descent ran out of budget. The last is still an upper bound worth keeping\n    but is not an optimality claim, which is the distinction the `provenance`\n    field in `solutions/` exists to preserve.\n\n    `order` is empty when `solve` was given an `upper` it never improved on; the\n    caller keeps whatever witness it already had in that case.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    value: int\n    order: list[int]\n    proof: str\n    nodes: int\n    seconds: float\n\n    @property\n    def proved(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self.proof)\n\n\ndef solve(\n    instance: MOSPInstance,\n    *,\n    upper: int | None = None,\n    upper_strategy: str = \"rule+cs-dfs\",\n    lower: int = 0,\n    max_nodes: int | None = None,\n    time_budget: float | None = None,\n    on_improve: \"Callable[[int, list[int]], None] | None\" = None,\n    **kwargs: object,\n) -> Solution:\n    \"\"\"Descend `k` with the complete search until it refutes or runs out.\n\n    Each satisfiable answer is re-simulated on the product order it induces\n    rather than trusted at `k`: the search's own measure can over-charge an\n    order no product sequence realises, so the witness is often worth more than\n    the `k` it was found at, and the next step starts from what it actually\n    achieves. That is also the point at which a wrong witness would be caught.\n\n    Args:\n        upper: where to start. Defaults to the `cs-dfs` heuristic bound.\n        lower: a known lower bound. Reaching it ends the descent with a proof\n            and no refutation, since nothing below it can be satisfiable.\n        max_nodes, time_budget: per-`k` budgets. An exhausted budget stops the\n            descent with `proved` false.\n        upper_strategy: the named strategy in `satisfiability.heuristics` that\n            supplies the starting upper bound when `upper` is not given. Every\n            stack it saves is a whole `decide` call the descent never makes, and\n            the calls it skips are the cheap ones at the top -- but it also\n            starts the expensive refutation nearer the optimum. `learned+cs-dfs`\n            starts lower than the `cs-dfs` default on 709 of the 6,376 corpus\n            instances (`reports/learning.md` §2.1).\n        on_improve: called with `(value, closing order)` each time the descent\n            lowers its bound. Without it a long run holds everything it has\n            found in memory until it returns, so an interrupted descent loses\n            all of it -- on a multi-day budget that is the difference between a\n            crash costing minutes and costing days.\n\n    Returns:\n        A `Solution` whose `order` is a customer closing order; pass it through\n        `product_order_from_customers` for a production sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    from satisfiability.heuristics import upper_bound\n\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    nodes = 0\n\n    if upper is None:\n        value, ordering = upper_bound(instance, upper_strategy)\n        order = _closing_order(instance, ordering)\n    else:\n        value = upper\n        order = []\n\n    if value <= lower:\n        return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", 0, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n    k = value - 1\n    while k >= lower:\n        deadline = None if time_budget is None else started + time_budget\n        answer = decide(instance, k, max_nodes=max_nodes, deadline=deadline, **kwargs)\n        nodes += answer.nodes\n\n        if answer.status == \"unsat\":\n            # The refutation says the optimum is above k. Claiming it *is*\n            # k + 1 needs a witness that achieves k + 1, and the witness in\n            # hand is only trusted at the value it simulates to. The search\n            # scores closing orders, not the product sequences they build, and\n            # the two agree on every instance measured -- but a proof that\n            # rests on \"measured\" rather than \"checked here\" is the kind this\n            # project has had to retract before.\n            if value == k + 1:\n                return Solution(value, order, \"refutation\", nodes,\n                                time.monotonic() - started)\n            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n        if answer.status == \"unknown\":\n            return Solution(value, order, \"\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n        found = answer.order\n        achieved = max_open_stacks(\n            instance, product_order_from_customers(instance, found))\n        if achieved < value:\n            value, order = achieved, found\n            if on_improve is not None:\n                on_improve(value, order)\n        k = min(k, achieved) - 1\n\n    # The descent walked down to the lower bound: nothing below it can be\n    # satisfiable, so no refutation is needed.\n    return Solution(value, order, \"bound\", nodes, time.monotonic() - started)\n\n\ndef _closing_order(instance: MOSPInstance, ordering: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"The closing order a product sequence induces, customers by last product.\"\"\"\n    position = {pattern: i for i, pattern in enumerate(ordering)}\n    active = [c for c in range(instance.n_customers) if instance.customer_patterns(c)]\n    return sorted(\n        active,\n        key=lambda c: (max(position[p] for p in instance.customer_patterns(c)), c),\n    )\n","numLines":556,"startLine":1,"totalLines":556}}}
